\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Attention represents relations \emph{pairwise}: $A_{ij}$ is a statement about one query and one
key. An important class is not of this form. In \emph{competitive exclusion} a set of variables
jointly bears on a target, its members compete for a finite influence budget, and which prevails
is settled by comparison \emph{within} the set --- the winner taking the bulk, close rivals
progressively less, everything past a content-decided cutoff exactly none. We prove that no
\emph{query-local} scheme --- rows computed independently per query, a class containing softmax,
every relative-position or geometric bias, and every elementwise gate --- can enforce any
non-product-form degree constraint, and that a second, independent obstruction stops any
row-normalized scheme from bounding \emph{every} key's fan-out below the query-to-key ratio,
which is why doubly stochastic attention does not help --- an obstruction that is severe wherever
a small key set serves many queries and weak in self-attention, where the separation theorem
carries the argument alone. \textbf{MarSea} --- \emph{multivariate relations by selective exclusion in attention} --- imposes
\emph{content-dependent degree constraints on both margins}, and on each margin it predicts
\emph{two} things rather than one --- a \textbf{capacity}, how much a node may commit or absorb,
and a \textbf{shape}, how that is distributed over its incident edges including which are exactly
zero. Each margin takes two steps: one inherits the mass an unmodified layer would allocate,
keeping an exclusive margin commensurate with a non-exclusive one, and one re-shapes that mass
over the relation at a \emph{predicted} exclusivity, producing exact zeros. \textbf{The bound we
claim is therefore on \emph{degree}, not on mass} --- how many candidates a node reaches inside
its relation, which is the quantity a query-local scheme provably cannot bound and a full-support
one cannot represent. \emph{Every predicted
parameter reads its participant together with the field that participant faces} --- the key with
its score column, the query with its incoming allocation --- so the coupling that defeats the
separation theorem is in the parameters as well as in the programs. Both are closed-form; neither
renormalizes rows; both run natively over the
visible prefix in a decoder. Read as retrieval of a ground-truth relation, the recovery interval
is exactly a \emph{precision--recall} interval with \emph{sharp} endpoints, one per error type,
and it is non-empty on every column with a positive margin --- so exact recovery is never
obstructed, and what a hard column costs is the \emph{width} of the target the head must hit.
This yields a falsifiable prediction for multi-hop evidence selection: with $m$ gold facts among
$n$ candidates, \emph{any} full-support attention has evidence precision exactly $m/n$ --- an
identity, not a tendency --- so it degrades as candidates are added and no training arrests it,
while MarSea's support size does not involve $n$. Our load-bearing experiment sweeps $m$ at fixed
task difficulty, separating the expressivity claim from the effect of exclusion-shaped training,
and we pre-commit to reporting either outcome. This concerns degradation with \emph{candidate
count} and not the positional U-shape, whose architectural origin is separately understood.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

An attention matrix is a weighted bipartite graph: $A_{ij}$ is an edge from key $j$ to query $i$.
Softmax decides which edges carry mass \emph{pairwise} --- each weight from one query and one key
--- under a single constraint per row, $\sum_j A_{ij}=1$.

Many relations are not pairwise. Our object is \textbf{multivariate competitive exclusion}: a set
$\mathcal{A}$ jointly bears on $b$, its members compete for a finite influence budget, and which
prevails is settled by comparison \emph{within} $\mathcal{A}$. Losing is graded near the top of
the order and absolute below it --- \emph{little to nothing}, not uniformly nothing. Competition
may be \textbf{ranked}, by a priority order over kinds, or \textbf{graded}, by a measured
quantity. We say \emph{exclusion} rather than \emph{prioritization} because ordering is not the
scarce capability --- row-softmax already ranks --- but \emph{ending} the order at a cutoff the
candidates themselves decide is.

\paragraph{One scene, both margins.} At a traffic intersection many signals may be present at
once --- lights, hand-held signs, pedestrians, an emergency vehicle, an officer --- under a
priority order that differs by jurisdiction and so must be learned from content. Two statements
follow, one per margin. \textbf{Preemption}: among the signals bearing on a lane the
highest-priority one renders the rest irrelevant (\emph{fan-in}). \textbf{Scope}: each signal
governs a \emph{set} of lanes, and how many varies by signal (\emph{fan-out}). Neither is a
property of any query--key pair; both concern a node's degree. The same structure appears
wherever a model must decide which of many candidates bear on a conclusion: which $m$ of $n$
retrieved passages support an answer, which instruction outranks which when they conflict.

\paragraph{Orientation.} Semantic influence ($\mathcal{A}\to b$) is not attention direction
(key $\to$ query), and nothing ties them: if the competing signals are keys, preemption is a
statement about a \emph{row}; if they are queries, the identical relation is a statement about a
\emph{column}. Which holds depends on how the representation is learned and may differ across
heads, so a design constraining one margin silently assumes an orientation it cannot verify.
MarSea constrains both.

\paragraph{Two obstructions, and MarSea.} Sec.~\ref{sec:theory} shows this is not a modelling
preference: any scheme computing each query's row from that query's own inputs --- softmax, the
relative-position and geometric-bias family, every elementwise gate --- provably cannot enforce
such a constraint however expressive its scoring function (Thm.~\ref{thm:separation}), and a
second, independent obstruction shows that no row-normalized scheme bounds every key's fan-out by
a common constant (Prop.~\ref{prop:conservation}). We therefore read $A$ as a relational graph
and impose \textbf{content-dependent degree constraints on both margins} --- \emph{degree} in
the weighted sense, the sum of incident edge weights, which is exactly what a budget constrains,
with cardinality control following from the exclusivity and exact under
Assumption~\ref{ass:margin}: for each key, a joint
program over its fan-out edges inherits the mass an unmodified layer allocates and redistributes
it over the relation at a predicted exclusivity $\tau_j$; for each query, a capped projection
does the same on the other margin at a predicted exclusivity $\tau_i$, beneath a ceiling fixed at
one unit. Every head reads its participant
\emph{and its field} --- $\tau_j$ and the gate see the score column, $\tau_i$ and its gate the
incoming allocation --- so no parameter of the mechanism is a function of a key or query alone.
The column
step is sparsemax \citep{martins2016softmax,peters2019sparse} --- strictly concave, exactly
solvable by sorting, non-iterative, unlike Sinkhorn attention \citep{sander2022sinkformers} or
capsule routing \citep{sabour2017dynamic} --- and the row step is a threshold, not a
normalization, which keeps Prop.~\ref{prop:conservation} from applying to it.

\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) A \textbf{change of represented object}, from the query--key pair
to a multivariate relation carried by \textbf{both margins} of the attention graph --- and on each
margin by \textbf{two} predicted quantities rather than one. A \textbf{capacity} says how much
influence a node may commit or absorb; a \textbf{shape} says how that influence is distributed
over its incident edges, \emph{including which edges are exactly zero}. Both are predicted from
content, and both run over the whole margin or a declared part of it
(Sec.~\ref{sec:method-gate}, App.~\ref{app:selective}). \textbf{Exclusivity is the pair}: a
capacity alone bounds a total without deciding membership, and a shape alone redistributes a total
the mechanism does not control --- which is why one margin, or one of the two quantities, is not
the object. Table~\ref{tab:object} places every mechanism we know of on these four cells;
\textbf{MarSea is the only entry that fills them}. The object is forced rather than chosen, by a
\textbf{separation theorem} and an independent \textbf{conservation obstruction} that together
require query coupling and a non-uniform column capacity.

\textbf{We claim the two shape cells and inherit the two capacities, and the disclaimer is
deliberate.} \citet{zhang2023mesh} learn \emph{both} marginals of a transport, each from its own
participant (App.~\ref{app:related}); a predicted capacity is theirs, not ours, and MarSea takes
its capacities from what an unmodified layer allocates --- which is what keeps an exclusive margin
commensurate with a non-exclusive one and makes $\sum_j c_j=n_q$ a consequence rather than an
anchor (Prop.~\ref{prop:twostep}). \textbf{What no prior mechanism does is predict the
\emph{shape}}: MESH's transport is entropy-regularized and its output dense, with the
regularization strength a global hyperparameter, so no edge is ever exactly zero and no per-key
parameter decides where the support ends --- and by
Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling} its column precision is therefore pinned at $m_j/n_q$ exactly
as row-softmax's is. Conversely the sparse-attention family predicts a shape query-locally, which
Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} shows cannot enforce a constraint on the other margin at all. The
shape parameters here read their participant \emph{together with the field it faces}, which
Cor.~\ref{cor:necessity} shows a participant-only parameter provably cannot substitute for.
(ii) A closed-form \textbf{mechanism} of five components realizing all four cells
(Sec.~\ref{sec:method}), with a native causal form in which prefix growth is provably
edge-removing. (iii) A \textbf{fidelity analysis} (Sec.~\ref{sec:fidelity}) recasting every
guarantee as precision and recall on \emph{both} margins --- a ceiling binding every full-support
mechanism irrespective of training, and a recovery interval with \emph{sharp} endpoints, one per
error type. (iv) A \textbf{prediction} for multi-hop evidence selection (Sec.~\ref{sec:apps}) and
a \textbf{protocol} (Sec.~\ref{sec:experiments}) whose load-bearing experiment separates the
expressivity claim from exclusion-shaped training and reports either outcome.


