\paragraph{Related work.} App.~\ref{app:related} treats all of it in full. \emph{Geometry in
scores} \citep{hu2018relation,wu2021rethinking,gao2021fast,liu2022dab,hou2024relation} is
query-local by Def.~\ref{def:qlocal}, so Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} covers it. So is
\emph{differential attention} \citep{ye2024differential}, which subtracts two row-softmax maps to
cancel over-allocation to irrelevant context; its weights are signed, so the theorem applies but
Cor.~\ref{cor:capacity} does not (App.~\ref{app:related}).
\emph{Transport-based attention}
\citep{sander2022sinkformers,shahbazi2025lotformer} and Slot Attention
\citep{locatello2020slot} identify the marginals as the design surface and fix the column
marginal to \emph{uniform}, equalizing fan-out at $n_q/n_k$ rather than bounding it by a
content-dependent constant; \citet{tay2020sparse} balances a block \emph{sorting} matrix rather
than the attention itself and is not in this group, while \citet{zhang2023mesh} \emph{does}
learn non-uniform marginals --- both of them --- and is the closest prior art to MarSea's
\emph{capacities} on either margin. \textbf{What it does not predict is a shape}: its transport
is entropy-regularized and dense at a global regularization strength, so it has no exact zeros
and Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling} pins its column precision at $m_j/n_q$ exactly as it does
row-softmax's (Table~\ref{tab:object}, App.~\ref{app:related}). \textbf{Selective Attention \citep{leviathan2024selective} is the one attention mechanism we know
of that escapes Thm.~\ref{thm:separation}}: its mask accumulates along the prefix, so what query
$i$ may give key $j$ depends on what \emph{earlier queries} decided about $j$. Expert-choice
routing \citep{zhou2022mixture} makes the same move outside attention, each expert selecting its
own top-$k$ tokens. Both decide on the receiving margin over a set of senders at once; neither
constrains a marginal, and neither predicts from content how much a key should emit --- which is
the step that turns coupling into a degree bound.
\emph{Sparse attention} \citep{martins2016softmax,peters2019sparse,correia2019adaptively} and the
regularized-argmax framework \citep{niculae2017regularized,blondel2019fenchel} supply our
variational form, which is \emph{not} our contribution; we depart from
\citet{correia2019adaptively} in predicting exclusivity per key \emph{from the key and its
column} rather than as a per-head constant, which Cor.~\ref{cor:necessity} argues is necessary.
Closest in motivation, \citet{velickovic2024softmax} prove that softmax attention must disperse
as the number of items grows and propose adaptive temperature as a remedy;
Prop.~\ref{prop:leakage} is that claim made quantitative on the fan-out margin, and
Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling} is why sharpening a full-support distribution cannot reach
$P_j=1$. \emph{Position bias} \citep{liu2023lost} is a distinct phenomenon with its own architectural
accounts \citep{chowdhury2026birth,wu2025emergence,herasimchyk2026residual}, which differ on how
much of it survives without positional encodings; we make no claim about that axis
(Sec.~\ref{sec:apps}).

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

With $n_q$ queries, $n_k$ keys, values $V_j$ and relational descriptors $\gamma_{ij}$, scores
combine content and relation:
\begin{equation}
s_{ij} = q_i\!\cdot\!k_j/\sqrt d + b(\gamma_{ij}).
\label{eq:scores}
\end{equation}
MarSea replaces the normalization step of an attention layer and has \emph{five} components,
introduced together so that the object analyzed in Sec.~\ref{sec:theory} is the one we propose;
App.~\ref{app:notation} collects notation and the guarantee each carries.

\subsection{Stage 1: the fan-out program}
\label{sec:method-fanout}
\textbf{The exclusive relation a key participates in is rarely its whole column.} A left-turn
arrow governs the left-turn lanes and is exclusive over nothing else. We therefore fix, per key,
an \textbf{exclusive set} $\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}\subseteq[n_q]$ --- decided from the content and
relation of the participants, and known before the program runs --- and the fan-out stage takes
\emph{two} steps, one per cell of the object.

\textbf{Step 1 fixes how much, and it is the layer's own attention.} With $A^{\mathrm{sm}}$ the
ordinary row-softmax of the scores over the visible pairs --- each \emph{query's} row normalized
over the keys it sees, $\sum_jA^{\mathrm{sm}}_{ij}=1$; no column is normalized here, the column
stage only \emph{reads} the column sums of this matrix ---
\begin{equation}
A^{\mathrm{sm}} \;:=\; \softmaxop_{\mathrm{row}}(S),
\qquad
c_j \;:=\; \textstyle\sum_i A^{\mathrm{sm}}_{ij},
\qquad
\bar c_j \;:=\; \textstyle\sum_{i\in\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}} A^{\mathrm{sm}}_{ij}.
\label{eq:step1}
\end{equation}
\textbf{The capacity is inherited, not predicted}, and it is inherited entrywise: every pair
outside the relation carries exactly what an unmodified layer gives it, so with an empty relation
the mechanism \emph{is} standard attention, not an approximation of it. Three things follow at no
cost. An exclusive column carries the same total as a non-exclusive one; every row's supply is
one unit before step 2 touches it; and $\sum_j c_j = n_q$ holds \emph{as a consequence} rather
than as an imposed anchor. (An earlier draft placed step 1 at a column softmax rescaled to
$c_j$; that preserved the total but not the entries, and an empty relation then reproduced
standard attention only in aggregate. We record the change because App.~\ref{app:numerics}
checks the entrywise identity.)

\textbf{Step 2 fixes which, and yields the exact zeros.} The exclusive set's quota $\bar c_j$ is
redistributed over that set alone,
\begin{equation}
p_{\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j},j} = \arg\max_{p \in \simplex^{|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|-1}}\big\{\langle p, \tau_j s_{\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j},j}\rangle + \Tent_\alpha(p)\big\},
\qquad
\tilde A_{\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j},j} = \bar c_j\, p_{\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j},j},
\qquad
\tilde A_{ij} = A^{\mathrm{sm}}_{ij}\ \ \forall\, i\notin\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}.
\label{eq:program}
\end{equation}
The relation is re-shaped and written back into the column standard attention produced; every
other entry of that column is untouched. The column total is therefore
\emph{exactly} $c_j$ whatever the exclusive set is, and mass can never be relocated off it
(Prop.~\ref{prop:twostep}). \textbf{What this buys and what it forgoes should be said together.}
A key can never emit \emph{more} into its relation than an unmodified layer would, and it can
never emit \emph{less} either: a distractor key keeps its whole softmax mass, and a large
$\tau_j$ concentrates that mass onto fewer members rather than shrinking it. Suppression of a
key's total is therefore not something this margin does; it decides membership, and what a
loser loses is reach, not mass. The row stage is where a query declines a key
(Sec.~\ref{sec:method-fanin}). Every result below is stated on the index set the program runs over,
so the unrestricted case $\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}=[n_q]$ is the special case and nothing is
reproved for it.

\textbf{Two limiting columns deserve a sentence each.} When exactly one member of the relation
is true, Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery} has $W_j=0$ and its interval is $[1/\delta_j,\infty)$: for every
$\tau_j\ge1/\delta_j$ the program puts the \emph{entire} quota $\bar c_j$ on the largest-scoring
member and an exact zero on every other, and because sparsemax is $1$-Lipschitz in its argument
the column moves \emph{continuously} in $\tau_j$ from spread to that allocation --- a hard
assignment reached through a continuous parameter, with the top gap of $s_{\cdot j}$, which
$\tauK$ reads, as the signal that the column has one target. When \emph{no} member is true the
program still allocates, a simplex having no empty point: such a key reaches whichever member
scores it highest, and the row stage is where that query declines it. The composition is what
matters. A distractor whose highest-scoring member is not the answer query is removed from the
answer row by Stage~1 alone, as an exact and permanent zero (Prop.~\ref{prop:stagecomp}), and
only distractors that concentrate \emph{onto} the answer query reach its row program at all;
App.~\ref{app:numerics} checks both, and E8 reports the split.
Here $\Tent_\alpha$ is the Tsallis $\alpha$-entropy, so \eqref{eq:program} is
$\alpha$-$\entmaxop(\tau_j s_{\cdot j})$. We fix $\alpha=2$, i.e.\ sparsemax, where the solution
is unique, exactly computable by sorting in $O(n_q\log n_q)$ with no fixed-point iteration, and
differentiable almost everywhere \citep{martins2016softmax,peters2019sparse}:
\begin{equation}
p_{ij} \;=\; [\,\tau_j s_{ij} - \psi_j\,]_+ ,
\label{eq:affine}
\end{equation}
affine in the score wherever positive and \emph{exactly} zero past the threshold $\psi_j$ --- the
precise sense of ``little to nothing''. The exclusivity $\tau_j$ \emph{selects between the two
regimes} rather than being tuned to one: support size is non-increasing in $\tau_j$, so a large
$\tau_j$ approaches ranked preemption and a small one graded competition, and one layer serves
both because it is predicted from the key \emph{together with the field it faces} --- one head
per margin, never one head used twice, the superscripts being load-bearing for reasons
App.~\ref{app:limitations} gives:
\begin{equation}
\tau_j = \tauK\big(k_j,\,s_{\cdot j}\big),
\qquad
\tau_i = \tauQ\big(q_i,\,\tilde A_{i\cdot}\big)
\ \ \text{(Sec.~\ref{sec:method-fanin})} .
\label{eq:tauhead}
\end{equation}
\textbf{Two things now place MarSea outside the query-local class, not one}: $\psi_j$ depends on
the whole column, and so does the parameter that sets it. Nor is reading $s_{\cdot j}$ a
convenience --- by Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery} the temperatures that recover exactly are
\emph{exactly} $[1/(W_j+m_j\delta_j),1/W_j)$, whose endpoints are \emph{functions of that
column} and whose width can be small, so a head seeing only $k_j$ cannot target an interval it
cannot observe.

\textbf{$\tau_j$ carries the whole fan-out claim, so it reads the statistic that answers it.}
With the capacity inherited, the only thing the mechanism decides on this margin is where the
support ends --- and by Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery} landing it means placing $\tau_j$ so that
$k^\star(\tau_j)=m_j$. The exclusivity head therefore takes, alongside the key and its score
column, the smooth \emph{effective cardinality}
$\nu_j := \|p_{\cdot j}\|_2^{-2} \in [1,|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|]$, which equals $k$ when
$p_{\cdot j}$ is uniform on $k$ atoms and, unlike $|\supp(p_{\cdot j})|$, carries a gradient.
Nothing is circular: computation runs
$s_{\cdot j}\to\tau_j\to p_{\cdot j}\to\nu_j$, and $\nu_j$ enters the next block or the next
layer's head as an input, never as a definition of $\tau_j$ within one solve. Whether $\nu_j$
carries $m_j$ at all is not an identity --- it depends on $\tau_j$ and on the column --- and
Fig.~\ref{fig:probe}(b) measures it.

\paragraph{What the two steps do and do not claim.} Every result in Sec.~\ref{sec:theory} uses
only $\bar c_j>0$, support recovery included. \textbf{We are explicit that the fan-out bound is now a
bound on \emph{degree} and not on \emph{mass}.} Step 1 hands the exclusive set whatever an
unmodified layer would have given it, so the mass a key emits into its relation is not a quantity
this mechanism predicts; what it decides is how many members of that relation receive any of it.
That is the cardinality half of Cor.~\ref{cor:capacity}, it is the half
Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} forbids a query-local scheme from enforcing, and it is what
Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling} compares against, since a full-support mechanism reaches
every candidate by construction. The step-1 total couples keys through $A^{\mathrm{sm}}$ and
introduces no dependence of one row on another row's inputs.

\subsection{Stage 2: the fan-in program}
\label{sec:method-fanin}
Stage 1 says nothing about what a \emph{query} receives, and the fan-in stage mirrors it exactly
--- an exclusive set $\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}\subseteq[n_k]$, a step that fixes how much, and a step
that fixes which. \textbf{Step 1} caps the whole row at the unit an unmodified layer spends,
\begin{equation}
a^{(1)}_{i\cdot} = \arg\min_{a\ge 0}\tfrac12\big\|a-\tilde A_{i\cdot}\big\|_2^2
\ \ \text{s.t.}\ \ \textstyle\sum_j a_j \le 1,
\qquad
\bar c_i := \textstyle\sum_{j\in\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}} a^{(1)}_{ij}.
\label{eq:rowstep1}
\end{equation}
This is the row as standard attention produced it, modified only where a column relation
re-shaped it, held beneath the unit that row already carried: every row of $A^{\mathrm{sm}}$ sums
to one, so the cap is the identity on any row no key has re-shaped, and binds exactly where a
fan-out concentration has \emph{added} mass to the row --- a condition on the relation's own
entries, which is what makes it well posed in finite precision (Prop.~\ref{prop:twostep} and the
remark after it). \textbf{It is not a second softmax.} The entries of $\tilde A$ are already
attention weights, and re-normalizing them would rescale the off-relation entries away from
standard attention's and --- because a Stage-1 zero is a weight of $0$, and a softmax of $0$ is
positive --- would resurrect every zero Stage 1 produced. The harness checks that the zeros
survive (Prop.~\ref{prop:twostep}(v)). \textbf{Step 2} re-shapes the relation's share beneath its
quota,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
A_{i,\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}} &= \arg\min_{a \ge 0} \tfrac12\big\|a/\tau_i - \tilde A_{i,\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}}\big\|_2^2
\ \ \text{s.t.}\ \ \textstyle\sum_{j\in\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}} a_j \le \bar c_i
\ \ \Longrightarrow\ \
A_{i,\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}} = \tau_i\,[\,\tilde A_{i,\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}} - \theta_i\,]_+ ,\\
A_{ij} &= a^{(1)}_{ij}\ \ \forall\, j\notin\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot},
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:rowprog}
\end{equation}
with $\theta_i\ge0$ the \emph{dual} of the quota, $\theta_i=0$ when the quota is slack and
otherwise solving $\sum_{j\in\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}}\tau_i[\tilde A_{ij}-\theta_i]_+=\bar c_i$.
As on the column, the relation is re-shaped and written back; the rest of the row is what step 1
left.

\textbf{The quota is a ceiling, not an equality, and the reason is a theorem we would otherwise
break.} With an equality in place of \eqref{eq:rowprog}'s inequality the target
$\bar c_i/\tau_i$ exceeds $\sum_{j\in\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}}\tilde A_{ij}$ whenever $\tau_i$ is small
enough, the dual goes negative, and every coordinate of the relation --- including entries Stage
1 set to \emph{exactly zero} --- becomes positive. That contradicts
Prop.~\ref{prop:stagecomp}(iv), which says a Stage-1 false negative is unrecoverable, and the
harness confirms it on roughly half of random rows. The inequality keeps $\theta_i\ge0$ for every
$\tau_i>0$ (Prop.~\ref{prop:twostep}(v)). \textbf{The solution has the same functional form as
the unrestricted program} --- one common floor and one common gain --- so every result stated in
terms of $\theta_i$ transfers with nothing reproved; only the equation fixing $\theta_i$ changes,
from a unit to the quota.

Write $\tilde R_i:=\sum_{j\in\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}}\tilde A_{ij}$ for the relation's supply on
row $i$. \textbf{The regime is set by $\tau_i\tilde R_i$ against the quota, not by $\tau_i$
alone.} For $\tau_i\le \bar c_i/\tilde R_i$ the ceiling is slack, $\theta_i=0$ and
$A_{i,\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}}=\tau_i\tilde A_{i,\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}}$: the support is untouched
and the query \emph{spends less than its quota} --- which softmax cannot do however irrelevant
every key is, and which $\softmaxop_1$ \citep{miller2023attention} supplies only
content-independently, by a fixed additive constant and without exact zeros;
\citet{xiao2024efficient} consider it and prefer a learnable sink. For $\tau_i>\bar c_i/\tilde R_i$
the ceiling binds, $\theta_i>0$, and the query spends exactly its quota concentrated on fewer
keys, with $\tau_i\to\infty$ admitting only $\arg\max_{j\in\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}}\tilde A_{ij}$.
\textbf{At $\tau_i=1$ step 2 returns exactly the row step 1 produced}: on a row the unit cap
left alone, $\bar c_i=\tilde R_i$ and the quota is met without a floor; on a row it bound, the
quota is what step 1's own floor left on the relation, and by uniqueness of the projection step 2
recovers that same floor (the harness confirms this on every cap-binding row). So $\tau_i=1$ is
the identity on $a^{(1)}$ --- the standard-attention start --- and the two regimes are read
against it: $\tau_i>1$ raises the floor and sharpens, $\tau_i<1$ lowers it and spreads. (An
earlier draft said $\tau_i=1$ was not an identity; that compared step 2 with $\tilde A$ rather
than with $a^{(1)}$, and it was wrong.) E8 reports the distribution of $\tau_i$ about $1$. This also scopes the second margin: the ceiling bounds fan-in
\emph{mass} unconditionally but fan-in \emph{degree} only where it binds, a slack row leaving
$\supp(A_{i,\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}})=\supp(\tilde A_{i,\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}})$ untouched. The
floor is common to a row but its \emph{value} is set by the whole relation --- the row-wise
analogue of the column coupling in \eqref{eq:program}, and the sense in which MarSea is
\emph{joint} on both sides. Crucially \eqref{eq:rowprog} is a constrained optimization but
\textbf{not a normalization}: the constraint is an inequality, and a row supplied with less
keeps less.

\textbf{Two honest notes on this margin.} First, the fan-in step is query-local by
Def.~\ref{def:qlocal} --- it is a function of the row and the keys --- so Thm.~\ref{thm:separation}
does not distinguish it from a row-entmax with a learned temperature, and any row-margin
comparison in Sec.~\ref{sec:experiments} is a comparison of \emph{which keys Stage 1 let reach
the row}, not of the row program itself (App.~\ref{app:limitations}). Second, when step 1's cap
binds it truncates the whole row at one floor, so a key's fan-out concentration on one column can
remove an off-relation entry from another query's row; the complement is fixed by step 1 in the
sense of Prop.~\ref{prop:twostep}(iv), but it is fixed \emph{after} that cap, not at the
unmodified layer's values.

\subsection{Conditional activation is the relation}
\label{sec:method-gate}
Not every key exercises exclusivity and not every query is contested, and imposing a degree
constraint on structure that has none is worse than wasteful. In an earlier draft this was a pair
of gates, $\omK$ on keys and $\omQ$ on queries, separate from the programs. \textbf{Under the
two-step form the gates are not separate components: they are the empty-relation case.} A key
with $\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}=\emptyset$ runs no program and keeps its softmax column entrywise; a
query with $\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}=\emptyset$ runs no program and keeps its step-1 row. So
\textbf{participation is a property of the relation, not of the participant alone}, exactly as
the motivation demands --- a signal preempts \emph{when competing signals are present} --- and it
is decided by the same pairwise compatibility that decides the relation
(App.~\ref{app:selective}): $e_{ij}=\mathbf{1}[\langle u_\phi(k_j),v_\phi(q_i)\rangle>0]$,
which reads the pair and induces both slices of one object.

Two consequences. Because $\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}$ is a slice of the same $\mathcal{E}$ as every
$\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}$, a query enters \eqref{eq:rowprog} \emph{exactly when} some key's
relation contains it; there is no second trigger and no separate row gate to tie to the column
one. And the degenerate optimum is now literal: \textbf{an empty relation everywhere is standard
attention}, entrywise, which App.~\ref{app:numerics} checks to machine precision. That optimum
is \emph{easier} to reach than any collapse of $\tau_j,\tau_i$, so the $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$
and $|\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}|$ distributions are diagnostics from epoch one and E8 scores relation
membership against annotated participation where it exists: a detector, not a scheme that avoids
it. Cost is unchanged, since $e_{ij}$ is a rank-$r$ inner product computed alongside the scores.
One position is excluded from every relation by construction: the first token of a decoder is
the attention sink, not a candidate for anything, and a query whose only visible key re-shapes
its quota away from it would be left with an empty row --- so $e_{i0}=e_{0j}=0$, and the sink's
row and column stay at standard attention.

\subsection{Scaling: blocking for incrementality, hierarchical selection for scale}
\label{sec:method-scale}
Two constructions extend \eqref{eq:program}. \textbf{Blocking} runs the program within tiles of
$B$ queries, making the per-step cost $O(n_kB\log B)$ --- linear in the key count, as attention
already is, with the program size and sort factor held at the block rather than growing with the
prefix. \textbf{Hierarchical top-$K$} addresses scale: since the sparsemax support is a
\emph{prefix} of the sorted order (Lem.~\ref{lem:prefix}), retaining the top $K$ per block and
solving one exact program at the root on \emph{original} scores reproduces the global solution
whenever $K\ge k^\star$ (Prop.~\ref{prop:tournament}). $K$ is a generous fixed constant set from
the measured $k^\star$ distribution (E8) rather than assumed. App.~\ref{app:scaling} has both in
full.

\subsection{Causal form: the program over a visible prefix}
\label{sec:method-causal}
In a decoder, the queries that may attend to key $j$ are those at positions $t \ge j$, and
MarSea's fan-out program runs over exactly that set --- a \emph{component}, not an adaptation,
since in a causal model there is no counterfactual ``full'' column that the prefix approximates:
which queries key $j$ governs is a question about the queries that exist. Every result in
Sec.~\ref{sec:theory} therefore applies verbatim, with $m_j,\delta_j,W_j$ computed over the
visible set $Q_j(t)$. What changes is that the column grows, and
Prop.~\ref{prop:prefix} records how: $\psi_j^{(t)}$ is non-decreasing, so \emph{exclusion is
permanent} while admission is provisional, and the incremental \emph{frozen-prefix} variant
over-admits and never under-admits. That monotonicity is at fixed $\tau_j$, so membership
and $\tau_j$ are \emph{sealed at the block boundary}, as the block's inherited quota
$\bar c^{(b)}_j$ is; a head re-reading a
growing column could move $\psi_j$ down and forfeit permanence. So the streaming implementation
need only ever \emph{evict}, and the frozen-prefix error is of the class Stage 2 can remove at
all rather than the one it provably cannot (App.~\ref{app:limitations}). We ablate both emission
policies.

\section{Theory}
\label{sec:theory}

\emph{Every claim in this section is proved in App.~\ref{app:proofs}, which restates each result
before proving it; the few given here in prose are stated formally there.}

\subsection{Two obstructions}

\begin{definition}[Query-locality, and product form]
\label{def:qlocal}
Write query $i$'s inputs as $x_i := (q_i,\gamma_{i\cdot}) \in \mathbb{R}^d\times\Gamma^{n_k}$
with admissible set $\mathcal{D}_i$. A scheme is \textbf{query-local} if for every $i$ the row
$A_{i\cdot}$ is a function of $(x_i,\{k_j\}_j)$ alone, independent of $x_{i'}$ for $i'\ne i$.
A constraint $\mathcal{C}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n_q}_{\ge0}$ is
\textbf{product-form} if $\mathcal{C}=\prod_i\mathcal{C}_i$; a capacity
bound $\sum_i x_i\le c$ is not, since whether one coordinate is admissible depends on the others.
The first is independence in the \emph{mechanism}, the second in the \emph{constraint}.
\end{definition}

Row-softmax, every additive relative-position or geometric bias and every elementwise gate
$A_{ij}=\alpha_{ij}g(q_i,k_j,\gamma_{ij})$ are query-local; MarSea is not.

\begin{theorem}[Query-local schemes attain exactly a product set]
\label{thm:separation}
Fix a key $j$, evaluate a query-local scheme over $\mathcal{D}=\prod_i\mathcal{D}_i$ with
$\{k_j\}$ fixed, and put $\mathcal{R}_i := \{A_{ij}(x_i) : x_i\in\mathcal{D}_i\}$. Then the
attainable set of fan-out columns is \emph{exactly} the product,
\begin{equation}
\big\{A_{\cdot j}(x) : x\in\mathcal{D}\big\} \;=\; \prod_{i=1}^{n_q}\mathcal{R}_i ,
\label{eq:product}
\end{equation}
and the scheme satisfies $A_{\cdot j}\in\mathcal{C}$ for all $x\in\mathcal{D}$ iff
$\prod_i\mathcal{R}_i\subseteq\mathcal{C}$. Hence no query-local scheme enforces a
non-product-form $\mathcal{C}$ except vacuously.
\end{theorem}

\begin{assumption}[Non-degenerate replication]
\label{ass:richness}
Fix key $j$. There exist $a>0$ and $M \le n_q$ such that some admissible input has at least $M$
queries $i$ with $A_{ij}(x_i)\ge a$. We call $(a,M)$ a \emph{replication pair} for key $j$.
\end{assumption}

\begin{corollary}[Cardinality fails, and fails on real inputs]
\label{cor:capacity}
Cardinality $\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{card}}=\{x:|\supp(x)|\le m\}$ with $1\le m<|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$
and capacity $\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{cap}}=\{x:\sum_i x_i\le c\}$ with $c>0$ are both
non-product-form for $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|\ge2$, so by Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} no query-local
scheme enforces either. The failure is realized on constrained input spaces: given a replication
pair $(a,M)$ inside the set (Assumption~\ref{ass:richness}), some admissible input has $M$
coordinates at least $a$, so \textbf{cardinality fails whenever $m<M$} and capacity whenever
$c<Ma$.
\end{corollary}

\textbf{MarSea claims the cardinality half and not the capacity half, and we say so here rather
than let it be inferred.} By \eqref{eq:step1} the mass a key emits into its relation is inherited
from an unmodified layer, so no \emph{budget} is predicted and no bound on
$\sum_{i\in\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}}\tilde A_{ij}$ is claimed. What the mechanism decides is
$|\supp(\tilde A_{\cdot j})|$, and that is the quantity the corollary shows a query-local scheme
cannot bound, that Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling} converts into a precision ceiling, and that
Fig.~\ref{fig:probe}(a) measures. One consequence is worth stating plainly: on each margin a
single parameter now carries the whole claim --- $\tau_j$ on the column, $\tau_i$ through
$\theta_i$ on the row --- where a predicted budget would have supplied a second, independent
constraint. That makes the interval-hit rate of Sec.~\ref{sec:experiments} the diagnostic rather
than one of several. It does \emph{not} make $\tau_j\to0$ a route back to standard attention:
by Lem.~\ref{lem:prefix} that limit is the \emph{uniform} allocation of the inherited quota
over the relation, a column re-shaped to the wrong shape rather than left alone, and the only
way back to standard attention is an empty relation (Prop.~\ref{prop:twostep}(vi);
App.~\ref{app:limitations}).

The hypothesis is generic rather than adversarial: wherever many candidates are near-equivalent
with respect to one key --- topically plausible distractors retrieved for the same question --- a
query-local gate, knowing nothing of the others, must treat them alike. \textbf{S0 measures
$(a,M)$ on trained-backbone columns} rather than assuming it; the probe already run is synthetic
(App.~\ref{app:expprotocol}).

\begin{proposition}[Conservation obstruction]
\label{prop:conservation}
If $\sum_j A_{ij}=1$ for every query, then $\max_j \sum_i A_{ij} \ge n_q/n_k$: no row-normalized
scheme can bound \emph{every} key's fan-out below the query-to-key ratio, and doubly stochastic
attention attains it with equality for every key.
\end{proposition}

Neither implies the other: Prop.~\ref{prop:conservation} forbids bounding all columns by a common
constant but not a single one, while Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} forbids a query-local scheme from
bounding even one. Together they force query coupling \emph{and} a column marginal that is
learned and non-uniform --- the total is conserved either way, and what MarSea changes is how it is
allocated, which is why a \emph{uniform} marginal does not address cardinality.

\paragraph{How hard the second bites depends on the shape of the attention, and the mechanism is
defined for all of them.} Both programs are stated for arbitrary $n_q$ and $n_k$, so MarSea
applies to encoder self-attention, to cross-attention, and to the decoder self-attention this
paper deploys in; what varies with the shape is not the mechanism but the \emph{strength} of
Prop.~\ref{prop:conservation}. Where a small key set serves many queries --- cross-attention,
encoder--decoder attention, any layer with $n_k \ll n_q$ --- the bound $n_q/n_k$ grows with the
query count and the obstruction is severe: no row-normalized scheme keeps any key's fan-out
bounded as queries are added. \textbf{In self-attention $n_q=n_k$ and the bound is the constant $1$, and we think it is weak
there.} Worse than weak: under causal masking the bound is attained at the first position, where
the only visible key is the first and $A_{11}=1$ before anything is trained, so in a decoder the
witness is an artifact of the mask rather than a statement about fan-out control. The proposition
remains true and it still forbids bounding \emph{every} column below one, but we do not lean on
it in the decoder, and a reader should not read the abstract's ``severe'' as applying to
Sec.~\ref{sec:apps}'s setting. \textbf{Nor does conditional activation rescue it.} The same
pigeonhole runs against MarSea's own inherited capacities: $\sum_j c_j = n_q$ over all keys gives
$\max_j c_j \ge n_q/n_k$, exactly as for the unmodified layer it inherits from. MarSea does not
escape conservation of the total, and never claimed to; what it changes is \emph{which} members
of a relation the total reaches, which a uniform column marginal cannot do.
\textbf{The load therefore shifts between the two obstructions with the shape.}
Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} is shape-independent, forbidding a query-local scheme from bounding
even one column at any $n_q$ and $n_k$, and it is what carries the argument in a decoder;
Prop.~\ref{prop:conservation} is what additionally rules out the transport-based remedy, and it
is strongest where the key set is small.

\subsection{What the two steps guarantee, and what shrinking a relation does}

\begin{proposition}[The two-step form conserves and cannot relocate]
\label{prop:twostep}
For every exclusive set $\mathcal{E}$ and every $\tau_j,\tau_i>0$:
\textbf{(i)} $\sum_i \tilde A_{ij} = c_j$ exactly, whatever $\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}$ is --- the
column carries the mass an unmodified layer gives it;
\textbf{(ii)} hence $\sum_j c_j = n_q$, as a consequence rather than an imposed anchor;
\textbf{(iii)} $\sum_j A_{ij}\le \sum_j A^{\mathrm{sm}}_{ij}$ for every query --- the mechanism
never gives a row more than the unmodified layer gave it, which is one unit in exact arithmetic
--- with equality only when step 1's cap binds, so a query may still spend less;
\textbf{(iv)} entries off $\mathcal{E}$ are fixed by step 1 and unchanged by step 2 on both
margins, so no margin claim can be met by relocating mass out of the relation;
\textbf{(v)} $\theta_i\ge0$ for every $\tau_i>0$, so a Stage-1 zero is never resurrected and
the relation's row mass never exceeds its quota $\bar c_i$;
\textbf{(vi)} with $\mathcal{E}=\emptyset$ the output equals $A^{\mathrm{sm}}$ entrywise.
\end{proposition}

\paragraph{Why (iii) is stated against the row's own mass, and what that buys in finite
precision.} In exact arithmetic $\sum_j A^{\mathrm{sm}}_{ij}=1$ and (iii) is the unit bound. We
write it against the row's own mass because the two are not the same number in floating point,
and the difference is a property of the \emph{reduction kernel} rather than of the mechanism: an
fp32 softmax row sums to $1+\delta$ with $|\delta|\le n_k\varepsilon/2$, the worst case being a
row with one dominant entry and a long tail lying just under half an ulp of it, so that a
$L$-lane reduction discards $1/L$ of that tail and $\delta$ reaches $n_k\varepsilon/(2L)$. That is
$\approx10^{-6}$ for a pairwise reduction and $6\times10^{-5}$ at $n_k=16{,}384$ for a $16$-lane
one. \textbf{Testing whether a row exceeds \emph{one} therefore makes the cap's binding set
device-dependent, and no fixed tolerance is correct on every kernel.} Testing whether the
\emph{relation} added mass does not: by (iv), $\tilde A=A^{\mathrm{sm}}$ off $\mathcal{E}$, so
\begin{equation}
\textstyle\sum_j \tilde A_{ij} - \sum_j A^{\mathrm{sm}}_{ij}
\;=\; \sum_{j\in\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}}\big(\tilde A_{ij}-A^{\mathrm{sm}}_{ij}\big)
\label{eq:excess}
\end{equation}
exactly --- a difference of in-relation terms that reads no row total. The implementation binds
\eqref{eq:rowstep1} on \eqref{eq:excess} and projects a binding row onto $\sum_jA^{\mathrm{sm}}_{ij}$,
so the binding \emph{decision} is a function of in-relation terms alone on every kernel, (v) and
(vi) hold \emph{exactly} --- an empty relation has excess bitwise $0.0$ and so reproduces standard
attention bitwise rather than to a tolerance. \textbf{What finite precision still costs is a
window, not a constant.} A row whose excess is positive but smaller than the projection's own
arithmetic can see is left uncapped, so the \emph{realized} row total may exceed
$\sum_jA^{\mathrm{sm}}_{ij}$ by that much: $10^{-7}$ on our reference CPU and $2\times10^{-6}$ at
$n_k=16{,}384$ on the deployment GPU, both measured. (iii) is a statement about the program and is
exact; the implementation meets it up to that window, which App.~\ref{app:numerics} reports and
which the release gates against the tolerance its invariants allow. App.~\ref{app:numerics}
likewise reports the kernel's own $\delta$ as a device statistic beside the checks rather than
inside them.

\begin{proposition}[Shrinking a relation only helps]
\label{prop:scopemono}
Let $T_j\subseteq\mathcal{E}'\subseteq\mathcal{E}$. Then $W_j$ and $m_j$ are unchanged, and
\textbf{(i)} $\delta_j(\mathcal{E}')\ge\delta_j(\mathcal{E})$;
\textbf{(ii)} the relative interval width $m_j\delta_j/W_j$ is non-decreasing;
\textbf{(iii)} if Assumption~\ref{ass:margin} holds on $\mathcal{E}$ it holds on $\mathcal{E}'$,
and the recovery interval on $\mathcal{E}$ is \emph{contained in} the one on $\mathcal{E}'$;
\textbf{(iv)} the converse fails --- Assumption~\ref{ass:margin} can hold on $\mathcal{E}'$ where
it fails on $\mathcal{E}$.
Restricting to a relation therefore never narrows the admissible temperature set and can create
one where none existed.
\end{proposition}

\noindent Part (iv) is the reason the restriction matters asymptotically rather than cosmetically.
$\delta_j$ is a minimum over the target set minus a maximum over its complement, so over a
complement that grows with the context it is non-increasing and converges to a limit that is
negative whenever a non-target can outrank the weakest target; past that point
Sec.~\ref{sec:recovery} is vacuous on the full column. Over a relation whose size does not track
the context it is context-free by construction. Table~\ref{tab:margin} measures both, and the
harness finds Assumption~\ref{ass:margin} failing on the full set but holding on the relation for
$11.2\%$ of columns drawn.

\subsection{Structure of the fan-out program}

The fan-out program has one structural property everything else rests on. With
$G(k):=\sum_{r\le k}(z_{(r)}-z_{(k)})$ non-decreasing in $k$, the support of
$\operatorname{sparsemax}(z)$ is exactly the $k^\star$ largest entries with
$k^\star=\max\{k:G(k)<1\}$: a \emph{prefix} of the sorted order (Lem.~\ref{lem:prefix}). Three
things follow. \emph{Monotone exclusivity}: $k^\star(\tau)$ is non-increasing, from $n_q$ at
$\tau\to0^+$ to $|\arg\max_i s_i|$ at $\tau\to\infty$. And the two facts Sec.~\ref{sec:method}
relies on --- exactness of hierarchical retention above $k^\star$
(Prop.~\ref{prop:tournament}) and permanence of exclusion as the causal prefix grows
(Prop.~\ref{prop:prefix}). $G$ also \emph{computes} the endpoints of Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery}.

\subsection{What is realizable, and where the difficulty lies}
\label{sec:competitive}

A bound on others is not a capability, so we give the positive counterpart. \emph{Every
competitive selection is realizable}: for any partition $\{T_j\}$ there are scores, exclusivities
and budgets making $\supp(A_{\cdot j})=T_j$ for every $j$ at once and at \emph{every} admissible
$\tau_i$ --- the floors are not free witnesses, being duals of the unit cap --- robustly to
sup-norm score perturbation below $1/2$, with cardinalities free apart from what a partition
forces (Prop.~\ref{prop:realizability}).

The negative half must be stated carefully, because one half of what we want \emph{is} available
to a query-local scheme (Prop.~\ref{prop:scope}). \textbf{(i)} Selection
at a \emph{single} target is: row-sparsemax at large temperature admits only $\arg\max_j s_{ij}$.
\textbf{(ii)} Scope --- a bound $|T_j|\le\kappa_j$ --- is not: a replication pair $(a,M)$ with
$\kappa_j<M$ drives $|\supp(A_{\cdot j})|\ge M>\kappa_j$ on some admissible input. \textbf{(iii)} The non-local content is
therefore neither in isolation but their \emph{conjunction}, which MarSea satisfies by
construction. Query-locality restricts a row's dependence on \emph{other queries'} inputs, not on
keys, so target-side exclusivity as such is not what is unavailable: what (ii) shows is that
\emph{independent} per-target selection over-commits a source
(App.~\ref{app:limitations}). ``The fastest lane'' is answerable one lane at a time; ``one vehicle per lane,
each taking the fastest lane still open to it'' is not. \textbf{This concession governs which
experiments count}: a gain at $m=1$ is not evidence for Thm.~\ref{thm:separation}.

\subsection{Exact recovery}
\label{sec:recovery}

\begin{assumption}[Margin and spread]
\label{ass:margin}
$\delta_j := \min_{i\in T_j}s_{ij} - \max_{i\notin T_j}s_{ij} > 0$; and
$W_j := \sum_{i\in T_j}(s_{ij}-\min_{i'\in T_j}s_{i'j})$, which is $\ge 0$ by definition rather
than by hypothesis.
\end{assumption}

\begin{theorem}[Exact support recovery, with sharp endpoints]
\label{thm:recovery}
Let $\alpha=2$ and Assumption~\ref{ass:margin} hold. Then $\supp(\tilde A_{\cdot j}) = T_j$
\emph{if and only if}
\begin{equation}
\tau_j \in \big[\,\tfrac{1}{W_j+m_j\delta_j},\ \tfrac{1}{W_j}\,\big)
\qquad(1/W_j := \infty \text{ if } W_j=0),
\label{eq:interval}
\end{equation}
and this interval is non-empty for \emph{every} column with a positive margin, since
$m_j\delta_j>0$.
\end{theorem}

Both endpoints are exact because $G(m_j{+}1)=\tau_j(W_j+m_j\delta_j)$ is computable rather than
merely bounded below. Exact recovery is therefore never obstructed on the column --- some
temperature always attains it --- and the obstruction is instead \emph{width}, the interval's
relative width being $m_j\delta_j/W_j$. Keys whose intervals are disjoint,
$W_{j_2}+m_{j_2}\delta_{j_2}\le W_{j_1}$, admit no common global $\tau$, so
\textbf{content-dependent exclusivity is necessary}, not merely convenient
(Cor.~\ref{cor:necessity}). Because the interval is a function of the score column the same
argument runs \emph{within} a key across contexts, which is why \eqref{eq:tauhead} reads
$s_{\cdot j}$. The two regimes fall out of the endpoints --- for $m_j=1$, $W_j=0$ and
any $\tau_j\ge1/\delta_j$ isolates the target; for large $m_j$ the upper endpoint binds and a
\emph{small} $\tau_j$ is needed --- and both occur in one instance.

\subsection{Fidelity: precision and recall on both margins}
\label{sec:fidelity}

Reading the allocation as retrieval of the ground-truth relation makes the guarantees legible and
locates where each error type enters. We carry \emph{both} margins throughout.

\begin{definition}[Support precision and recall, both margins]
\label{def:fidelity}
For key $j$ with target set $T_j$, $m_j=|T_j|$ and $S_j := \supp(p_{\cdot j})$, and query $i$
with true \emph{source} set $K_i$ and $S_i := \supp(A_{i,\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}})$ --- the
support \emph{on the relation}, since off it the row carries the unmodified layer's dense entries
by construction and precision there is not a quantity the mechanism decides --- put
$P_j = |S_j\cap T_j|/|S_j|$, $R_j = |S_j\cap T_j|/m_j$, $\rho_j = \sum_{i\in T_j} p_{ij}$;
$P_i = |S_i\cap K_i|/|S_i|$, $R_i = |S_i\cap K_i|/|K_i|$, with the convention that precision is
$1$ on an empty support. Where the two stages must be distinguished we write
$S_j^{(1)} := \supp(\tilde A_{\cdot j})$ and $S_j^{(2)} := \supp(A_{\cdot j})$, and likewise
$S_i^{(1)}, S_i^{(2)}$ on the row, all taken on the relation; $S_j^{(1)}=S_j$ since $\bar c_j>0$.
Where $T_j\not\subseteq\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}$ the excluded targets are recall misses before any
program runs, and we report \textbf{relation recall} $|T_j\cap\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|/m_j$
separately so that a mask error is never charged to a temperature.
\end{definition}

Under any \emph{normalized} allocation mass-precision and mass-recall coincide at $\rho_j$: the
two error types become definable only once exact zeros exist, so this is a capability a fully
supported mechanism lacks, not a vocabulary.

\begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.315\textwidth}
\vspace{-17pt}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.29\textwidth]{pr_curve.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{The trace is predicted in full.} Stage 1 at $400$ temperatures, one column,
$n_q\!=\!64$, $m_j\!=\!4$. Full support is the grey square, at \emph{every} $\tau_j$.}
\label{fig:prcurve}
\vspace{-15pt}
\end{wrapfigure}

\emph{Full support pins precision, on both margins} (Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling}). If a
mechanism assigns $p_{ij}>0$ to every pair --- row-softmax, any additive bias on it, any
elementwise gate with positive factors --- then for every key, every score configuration and
every temperature, $P_j = m_j/n_q$ and $P_i = |K_i|/n_k$ \emph{exactly}. Both are independent of
the scoring function, so neither improves by training, by architecture within the class, or by
any temperature; both tend to $0$ as the candidate set grows, while recall is $1$ trivially.

This is not a technicality about how support is defined: a mechanism whose output set is fixed in
advance has surrendered precision as a \emph{controllable} quantity, the scores being free to
rank the targets perfectly while the precision is unchanged. Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} says a
query-local mechanism cannot enforce the constraint and Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling} that
a full-support one cannot represent the answer; both must be cleared, which is why MarSea couples
and zeroes.

The interval of Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery} is itself a precision--recall interval
(Prop.~\ref{prop:printerval}), and because both endpoints are exact so is the correspondence
--- Fig.~\ref{fig:prcurve}, with $\rho_j=1$ inside as well. The interval is never empty, so the
obstruction is not attainability but \emph{width}: a column with large within-set spread leaves a
narrow target, which is what makes a head reading that column necessary rather than merely
helpful. Outside it the failure is graded rather than abrupt, each margin shedding true members
in non-decreasing order of strength, ties together, at a rate exact in $\tau_j$ on the column and
in $\theta_i$ on the row, so the two are separately diagnosable (Prop.~\ref{prop:graded}).

The two stages compose in one direction only (Prop.~\ref{prop:stagecomp}): Stage 2 only shrinks
supports, so it only loses recall and a Stage-1 false negative is \textbf{unrecoverable}, while
an \emph{exact} precision once achieved is preserved through any admission floors whatsoever. The
composition is \textbf{ratcheted}, not cancelling --- $\theta_i$ is a row threshold and $P_j$ a
column quantity, so Stage 2 may also lower a column's precision, which is why E7 reports both
readings rather than attributing a deficit by subtraction. Recall is the fragile side on both
margins --- at least $3\!:\!1$ on the column, $2\!:\!1$ on the row
(App.~\ref{app:limitations}) --- so no single $F$-style aggregate should be reported. The stage \emph{order} is settled by theorem,
precision-preservation being available only in this order, which is what makes frozen-prefix safe.

\begin{corollary}[Both quantities, both margins, simultaneously]
\label{cor:joint}
Let Assumption~\ref{ass:margin} hold for every key, and let the two ground truths be
\emph{consistent}, $K_i=\{j : i\in T_j\}$. If $\tau_j\in[\frac{1}{W_j+m_j\delta_j},\frac{1}{W_j})$
and $K\ge m_j$ for every $j$, and
$\theta_i < \min_{j\,:\,i\in T_j} c_j(1-\tau_jW_j)/m_j$ for every $i$, then $P_j=R_j=1$ on every
column and $P_i=R_i=1$ on every row, all four at once. Consistency is what makes ``both margins
at once'' well posed: without it a key may allocate to a query that does not count it as a
source, and no floor common to the row removes that edge while keeping the true ones.
\end{corollary}

\textbf{This is not a point on a trade-off curve.} Under Assumption~\ref{ass:margin}, sweeping
$\tau_j$ traces the \textbf{L} of Fig.~\ref{fig:prcurve} --- corner at $P_j=R_j=1$, \emph{no
interior point}, $R_j=1$ with $P_j=m_j/k^\star$ below the interval and $P_j=1$ with
$R_j=k^\star/m_j$ above (Lem.~\ref{lem:prefix} gives $|S_j|=k^\star$) --- while a full-support
mechanism does not move from $(1,m_j/n_q)$ at any temperature. Recall is therefore not what
precision is bought with, and it is given exactly rather than bounded. The conditions are not
prices paid, but nor are they free: $\tau_j$ must be \emph{found} in an interval never empty but
possibly narrow, $K\ge m_j$ is what E8 protects, and $\theta_i$ is reachable only through
$\tau_i$. E7 measures all three, and the curve.

Smoothing is not a way around a large candidate set either. Were the fan-out computed by
column-\emph{softmax}, holding the mass outside the target set relative to that inside at
$\varepsilon$ would take $\tau_j\ge\frac{1}{\delta_j}\log\frac{n_q-m_j}{m_j\varepsilon}$, in the
worst case over configurations meeting Assumption~\ref{ass:margin} and with the bound tight there
--- growing like $\log n_q$, whereas exact recovery at $\alpha=2$ needs
$\tau_j\ge1/(W_j+m_j\delta_j)$, with no $n_q$ displayed (Prop.~\ref{prop:leakage}; on what
survives inside $\delta_j$, App.~\ref{app:selective}).

\textbf{Smoothing does not relax a large candidate set --- it is what makes it bite}, since every
additional non-target contributes its own small share while a terminated tail removes the
dependence outright. Sec.~\ref{sec:apps} turns that into a measurement.

\section{Application: multivariate evidence selection}
\label{sec:apps}

Deciding which $m$ of $n$ retrieved passages support an answer is a joint selection among
topically plausible distractors: $m>1$ with coupling across targets, not the case
Prop.~\ref{prop:scope}(i) concedes. Three results meet there: by
Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling} any full-support attention has evidence precision exactly
$m/n$, falling to zero as candidates are added; by Prop.~\ref{prop:leakage} the temperature
needed to hold leakage fixed grows like $\log n$ in the worst case; and MarSea's support size
involves no $n$ on the relation --- a candidate outside it keeps its full-support weight, so the
ceiling is removed only on the covered part of a row, and E3 reports the coverage rate
$|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|/n$ alongside.
\textbf{The theory therefore predicts that evidence selection degrades with candidate count for
architectural reasons, and that MarSea's does not.} The setting is a causal decoder, native by
Sec.~\ref{sec:method-causal} rather than a reformulation.

\textbf{What we are not claiming}: long-context degradation also has a \emph{positional}
component with its own account \citep{chowdhury2026birth,wu2025emergence,herasimchyk2026residual},
which E3 controls for and App.~\ref{app:limitations} separates from ours in full.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

Every claim is matched to a measurement that could falsify it, and \textbf{E3 is load-bearing}:
it, not aggregate accuracy, decides whether the theory is doing the work.
\paragraph{Setup.} We fine-tune a borrowed $1$--$3$B decoder --- Qwen2.5-1.5B (Apache 2.0)
primary, Llama-3.2-3B as a cross-family check --- with MarSea replacing \emph{the softmax line of
the attention step and nothing else} in the layers that carry the backbone's retrieval heads
\citep{wu2024retrieval}, $\tauK,\tauQ$ and the two relation heads as small modules reading a key
or query together with a streaming summary of its column or row, and compare against \emph{the
same decoder, the same layers, the same adapters and the same schedule with unmodified
attention} --- a mechanism ablation, not a leaderboard entry. Every baseline is the same
substitution at the same line. Since MarSea's claim concerns the number of \emph{candidates}, not raw token
length, sweeps run at moderate context ($2$--$16$K tokens) with $n$ up to a few hundred passages;
App.~\ref{app:expprotocol} gives the reporting rules.


\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{v1_probe.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{E1, the replication probe.} \textbf{(a)} The true relation size is held at
$m_j=4$ and the candidate set $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ grows from $4$ to $256$; $12$ seeds,
$\tau_j$ taken inside Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery}'s interval. \textbf{The measured quantity is
degree, not mass}: by \eqref{eq:step1} the mass is inherited from an unmodified layer, so a mass
sweep would show MarSea tracking softmax and would be the wrong instrument. Solid, left axis:
fan-out degree. Any full-support scheme reaches every candidate (slope $+1$); MarSea holds
$4.00$ throughout (slope $-0.0000$), which is Cor.~\ref{cor:capacity}'s cardinality half.
Dashed, right axis: precision, which for the comparator is
$P_j=m_j/|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ at \emph{every} temperature and scoring function
(Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling}) --- an identity, not a tendency --- and $1.000$ for MarSea.
\textbf{(b)} The one link that is not an identity. With the capacity inherited, $\tau_j$ carries
the whole fan-out claim and must place $k^\star$ at $m_j$; its head reads
$\nu_j=\|p_{\cdot j}\|_2^{-2}$, and whether $\nu_j$ tracks $m_j$ depends on $\tau_j$ and the
column. Six keys, true sizes $1$--$32$ at $n_q=63$, error bars one standard deviation. Under a
per-key $\tau_j$ inside its interval it tracks; under one global $\tau$ it saturates, which is
Cor.~\ref{cor:necessity} in training-free form. Both panels are training-free possibility
results. Reproduced by \texttt{v1\_probe.py}.}
\label{fig:probe}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Datasets, and what each is for.} \textbf{RULER} \citep{hsieh2024ruler} is the
\emph{identifying} instrument, needle and distractor counts both being configuration parameters
so that $m$ moves \emph{at constant task difficulty}; \textbf{MuSiQue}
\citep{trivedi2022musique} is the \emph{externalising} task, with \textbf{HotpotQA}
\citep{yang2018hotpotqa} secondary and never alone; and \textbf{IHEval} \citep{zhang2025iheval}
supplies what evidence selection cannot, a \emph{ranked} order over conflicting instruction
tiers. App.~\ref{app:expprotocol} gives the reasons, including why neither instrument is
reported alone.


\paragraph{Baselines.} Six, each isolating one design decision and retuned under a matched
schedule rather than quoted. Two are decisive and they ablate different things.
\textbf{A learned-marginal transport in the MESH form} ablates \emph{capacity against shape}: it
predicts both marginals and no shape, which is the mirror image of what we do, and
Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling} predicts its column precision is pinned at
$m_j/|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ regardless. \textbf{An exclusivity head reading $k_j$ alone},
$\tauK(k_j)$ with the column statistic withheld, ablates the \emph{field argument};
Cor.~\ref{cor:necessity} predicts it fails wherever two keys need disjoint intervals, and
Fig.~\ref{fig:probe}(b) is the training-free form of the same test
(App.~\ref{app:expprotocol}).

\paragraph{E1 --- the replication probe (run).} \textbf{The probe measures degree, not mass},
for the reason Sec.~\ref{sec:method-fanout} gives: step 1 hands the relation whatever an
unmodified layer would, so mass is not the bounded quantity and a mass sweep would show MarSea
tracking softmax. Fig.~\ref{fig:probe}(a) holds the true relation size at $m_j=4$ and grows the
candidate set $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ from $4$ to $256$. MarSea's fan-out degree is
$4.00$ at every size --- slope $-0.0000$ --- while any full-support scheme reaches all
$|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ of them, slope $+1.0000$. Precision follows as an identity rather than
a tendency: $1.000$ against $1.000, 0.500, 0.250, 0.125, 0.062, 0.031, 0.016$. \textbf{That gap is an identity on the comparator's side}: by
Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling} a mechanism with $p_{ij}>0$ everywhere has
$P_j = m_j/|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ at \emph{every} temperature and every scoring function, so no
training, no additive bias and no elementwise gate moves it. It is Cor.~\ref{cor:capacity}'s
cardinality half on MarSea's side: $\tau_j$ from Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery}'s interval terminates the
tail, and the interval is non-empty on every column with a positive margin.

\textbf{What panel (b) adds is the one link that is not an identity.} With the capacity
inherited, $\tau_j$ carries the whole fan-out claim, and landing it means placing $k^\star(\tau_j)$
at $m_j$; the head reads $\nu_j = \|p_{\cdot j}\|_2^{-2}$, and whether $\nu_j$ tracks the true
$m_j$ depends on $\tau_j$ and on the score column rather than following from the definitions. It
does under a per-key $\tau_j$ inside its interval --- $1.00, 1.59, 3.20, 6.68, 13.95, 28.21$
against true sizes $1$ to $32$ --- and saturates under one global $\tau$, at
$1.34, 2.09, 3.66, 5.82, 8.11, 11.48$, compressing a factor of $32$ into a factor of $8.6$. That
is Cor.~\ref{cor:necessity} in training-free form, and the reason a uniform-exclusivity null
cannot supply the head with a usable input at all. \emph{Both panels remain possibility results}:
$\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}$ and $\tau_j$ are set by hand and Stage 2 is inert, so they show what a
column-coupled mechanism \emph{can} express where query-local schemes provably cannot, not that a
trained $\tau^{K}$ will find it. S0 is the measurement (App.~\ref{app:expprotocol}).

\paragraph{E2 --- the $m$-sweep at fixed $n$ (a Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery} test).} On RULER, hold
$n$ and sequence length fixed and sweep the needle count $m\in\{1,2,4,8,16\}$. \textbf{An earlier
draft called this load-bearing and read a precision gap growing with $m$ as evidence for
Thm.~\ref{thm:separation}. That reading is arithmetically inverted}: by
Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling} the comparator's evidence precision is the identity $m/n$,
which \emph{rises} with $m$ at fixed $n$, so MarSea's precision gap $1-m/n$ \emph{shrinks} with
$m$ if MarSea is at ceiling, and could only grow if MarSea failed at small $m$. What growing $m$
stresses is the recovery interval, whose endpoints both scale like $1/m$: the head must land a
narrower absolute target. E2 therefore reports MarSea's own precision, exact-set accuracy and the
\emph{interval-hit rate stratified by $m$}, and its falsification is a hit rate falling with $m$.
The comparator arm carries no information here beyond confirming the identity.

\paragraph{E3 --- the $n$-sweep at fixed $m$ (load-bearing).} Hold $m$ fixed and sweep the
candidate count $n$. \emph{This is the experiment the paper lives on}, and it is
Fig.~\ref{fig:probe}(a) on real data: the comparator's degree grows as $n$ and its precision
falls as $m/n$ (Cor.~\ref{cor:capacity}'s cardinality half and Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling}),
while MarSea's degree stays at $k^\star=m$ \emph{on the columns where Assumption~\ref{ass:margin}
holds}. That qualifier is the test. If the relation is the whole candidate set, App.~\ref{app:selective}
predicts the assumption fails on a growing fraction of columns as $n$ grows
(Table~\ref{tab:margin}: $63.6\%$, $14.9\%$, $1.2\%$ recovery at $32$, $128$, $512$), so E3
reports, per $n$, the fraction of active columns with $\delta_j>0$, the interval-hit rate on
those, and MarSea's precision on those against on all; \textbf{a flat precision on the
$\delta_j>0$ columns beside a falling fraction of such columns is the theory's own prediction},
and a flat precision on \emph{all} columns would mean the relation is doing the selection rather
than the program (App.~\ref{app:selective}, cost (a)). \emph{Both outcomes are reportable and we
pre-commit to reporting whichever we observe.}

\paragraph{E3, continued --- controls and what is reported.} E3 reports supporting-fact
precision and recall and answer F1 against $n$, alongside the measured relation-coverage rate
$|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|/n$. At the \emph{support} level the comparator's precision is
$m/|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ with no seed variance, an identity; at the \emph{task} level a
softmax decoder can emit exactly $m$ items, so no such identity holds and we make no
no-variance prediction there. Two controls are mandatory, since without both an $n$-effect is
indistinguishable from a length- or position-effect: every point runs twice against inert padding
of equal token count \citep{du2025context}, and gold sits at a fixed relative position with the
position-marginal profile reported alongside \citep{chowdhury2026birth}. App.~\ref{app:expprotocol}
gives the grids and the aggregation: which layers carry MarSea, which head defines the row, and
that a passage's support is the union over its token keys.

\paragraph{E4--E9 --- controls, externalisation, fidelity, diagnostics, ablations.} E4 repeats E2
at $m=1$ (RULER S-NIAH), the regime Prop.~\ref{prop:scope}(i) concedes, where a \emph{large} gap
is the interesting negative; E5 externalises to MuSiQue and HotpotQA-distractor,
corroborating E2, never replacing it; E6 tests the \emph{ranked} half on IHEval, reporting
accuracy against conflicting-tier count as a slope, not a pooled mean. Where gold evidence is annotated, E7
reports all five quantities of Def.~\ref{def:fidelity} --- never a single aggregate --- recall
\emph{twice}, the measured trace against Fig.~\ref{fig:prcurve}, and the \textbf{interval-hit
rate}: the fraction of active columns whose realized $\tau_j$ satisfies \eqref{eq:interval}.
\textbf{That last is the sharpest single test of the theory}, being the one quantity the theory
does not already determine, and it is available only because $\tauK$ reads that column. E8 logs the
relation's coverage on both margins, the $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ and $|\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}|$
distributions and the realized $k^\star$; E9 ablates every design decision,
\textbf{including each head's field argument and the tying of $\tauK$ to $\tauQ$}; the
field-argument ablation is contribution (i)'s load-bearing test, and its capacity-against-shape
arm is a published mechanism \citep{zhang2023mesh}, not a construction of ours. All three are in
App.~\ref{app:expprotocol}, and E1--E9 together are staged cheapest-first at $\approx336$
H100-hours with a \emph{pre-committed pivot} per stage (Table~\ref{tab:compute}).

\paragraph{Limitations.} The guarantees are single-layer, concern \emph{support} not the layer
output, hold only on the relation, and fidelity needs an annotated relation;
App.~\ref{app:limitations} has these and the rest in full;
App.~\ref{app:selective} frees Assumption~\ref{ass:margin} from the candidate count.
\textbf{Apart from E1, Sec.~\ref{sec:experiments} has not been run.}

\subsection*{Reproducibility statement}
All analytical claims are stated with explicit hypotheses and proved in App.~\ref{app:proofs};
each is additionally checked by a self-contained numpy program supplied as supplementary
material, which seeds each block separately, runs in under $25$ seconds on one CPU core, and
reproduces every figure in Tables~\ref{tab:verification} and \ref{tab:transferchecks}, rows
marked ``each'' being printed one per case.
\textbf{Every claim verifies at zero violations}, with the count reported \emph{per claim} in
Tables~\ref{tab:verification} and \ref{tab:transferchecks} rather than as one headline number,
because the rows are not commensurable and not all of them could have failed: a dagger marks
every row whose conclusion follows from how the generator builds its input, a double dagger every
row that re-expresses evaluations already counted elsewhere, and App.~\ref{app:numerics} says
what each does and does not test. \textbf{What the harness catches is algebra errors, off-by-one
errors in sorted-order arguments, and hypotheses that are accidentally stronger than intended} ---
which is how the non-sharp form of Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery} was found. It is not evidence about
real data, and the interval width on a trained network is E7. No block discards a column for
failing a claim: the sharp interval is non-empty on every column drawn, so the two blocks that
previously reported \emph{accepted} counts now sample the interval directly; three rows testing
the regime above $1/W_j$ are defined only where $W_j>0$ and count accordingly. E1 is synthetic and
training-free; its construction is in App.~\ref{app:expprotocol}. All datasets named in
Sec.~\ref{sec:experiments} are public.

\subsection*{AI use statement}
Large language model assistance was used for literature search, LaTeX editing, and consistency
checking. All theorem statements, proofs, and experimental design decisions are the authors';
every claim retained was verified by the authors, and the numerical verification was executed and
its output inspected rather than reported from a model's description of it.

