\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

\section{Blocking and hierarchical selection}
\label{app:scaling}
Two constructions extend \eqref{eq:program}; they address different obstacles and conflating
them has misled us once. \textbf{Blocking} partitions queries into blocks of size $B$ and runs
the program within the tile, with key $j$'s per-block quota $\bar c^{(b)}_j$ inherited from the
block's own softmax over its visible queries, so that $\sum_j c_j^{(b)}=|Q_b|$ holds by the same
consequence as \eqref{eq:step1}; admitting a query perturbs only its own block, so the per-step
cost is $O(n_kB\log B)$ against $O(n_kt\log t)$ unblocked. Note that ``perturbs only its own
block'' is a statement about blocks, not about keys: admitting a query changes the row-softmax
of that query and hence every $c_j^{(b)}$ in the block, which is what a streaming implementation
recomputes at each boundary. Both are linear in
the key count --- in a decoder $n_k$ \emph{is} the context length, so blocking does not make the
step cost independent of it; what blocking removes is the growth of the program size and of the
sort factor with the prefix, replacing $t\log t$ by $B\log B$. What remains is emission,
not compute. \textbf{Hierarchical top-$K$} addresses scale: because the sparsemax support is a
\emph{prefix} of the sorted order (Lem.~\ref{lem:prefix}), retaining the top $K$ scores per
block, merging survivors recursively and solving one exact program at the root on
\emph{original} scores reproduces the global solution exactly whenever $K\ge k^\star$
(Prop.~\ref{prop:tournament}), in $O(n_q)$ time and $O(K)$ memory per column. Intermediate
entmax is unnecessary: lower levels perform selection only, and running entmax at each computes
a normalization the next discards.

$K$ should be a generous fixed constant, \emph{not} a learned per-block budget: a learned count
makes a discrete commitment no higher level can undo, adding a recall failure path that no
higher level can repair, and buys nothing $\tau_j$ does not already give. How generous is
regime-dependent, and the qualification is doing work: over $4000$ random columns with
$n_q\le2048$ (App.~\ref{app:numerics}), $k^\star$ has $99$th percentile $5$ and maximum $8$ when
the column is exclusive but reaches $74$ when columns are deliberately broad. A fixed $K$ in the
tens is therefore safe \emph{on exclusive columns}, not universally. Because membership is
learned (Sec.~\ref{sec:method-gate}) the relation \emph{can} come to admit only well-separated
columns, which are exactly the ones where $k^\star$ is small, so the hierarchy would be asked to
be exact only where exactness is cheap. We do not assume it does: the relation is learned, not
constructed, so the interaction is available rather than guaranteed. E8 reports the realized
$k^\star$ stratified by $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$, and its falsification clause is what protects
the hierarchy in the meantime.

\section{Related work in full}
\label{app:related}\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Geometry in scores, and other query-local remedies.} Relation modules over box
geometry, directional and contextual relative-position encodings, content-predicted spatial
modulation, anchor-modulated positional attention and explicit position-relation priors
\citep{hu2018relation,wu2021rethinking,gao2021fast,liu2022dab,hou2024relation} all inject
relational information into the \emph{score}. Every one is query-local by Def.~\ref{def:qlocal},
so Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} applies to all of them, and MarSea is complementary rather than
competing: relational information still enters the score through $b(\gamma_{ij})$, and what
changes is that the allocation is then decided jointly per key. The same applies to a more recent
remedy aimed at our own motivating symptom. \citet{ye2024differential} take the diagnosis that
attention over-allocates to irrelevant context and subtract two row-softmax maps so that the
common-mode part cancels. Each map is a function of its own row, so the difference is query-local and the attainable-set
identity of Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} covers it. \textbf{Our other two instruments do not
transfer to it}, and it is worth being exact about why: the weights are \emph{signed}, so
Cor.~\ref{cor:capacity}'s realization argument, which lower-bounds a column sum by $Ma$ from a
replication pair with $a>0$ over the non-negative orthant, has no force where a column sum can be
small by cancellation; and each row sums to $1-\lambda$ rather than $1$, so
Prop.~\ref{prop:conservation} applies only with that constant in place of one. It is not an
allocation in the sense used here, and the fidelity quantities of Sec.~\ref{sec:fidelity} are
not defined on it without modification. It is a good demonstration that the phenomenon is real and
worth a mechanism, and it is a natural addition to the matched-sparsity baseline group of
App.~\ref{app:expprotocol}.

\paragraph{Transport-based attention, and Slot Attention as its closest relative to Stage 1.}
Sinkformers replace row-softmax with Sinkhorn normalization to obtain doubly stochastic attention
\citep{sander2022sinkformers}, following earlier use of Sinkhorn balancing for sparse attention
\citep{tay2020sparse}, and recent work makes it efficient \citep{shahbazi2025lotformer}.
\citet{tay2020sparse} is in fact a different construction --- Sinkhorn is applied to a block
\emph{sorting} matrix and attention over the reordered blocks remains an ordinary row-softmax ---
so the group that actually constrains the attention matrix is \citet{sander2022sinkformers} and
\citet{shahbazi2025lotformer}. That group identifies the marginals as the surface governing mass
allocation and then sets the column marginal to \emph{uniform}: every key emits identically. Slot Attention
\citep{locatello2020slot} belongs here more squarely than its usual framing suggests, and it
repays being read in this paper's orientation: its slots emit the queries and its inputs supply
the keys, so slot $=$ query and input $=$ key. It normalizes twice. The \emph{first}
normalization is a softmax over slots, which in these indices is
$\sum_i \mathrm{attn}_{ij} = 1$ for every input --- \textbf{a column marginal in exactly the
sense of Sec.~\ref{sec:method}, fixed uniformly at one}, and the closest published relative of
Stage 1. The second divides by the sum over inputs, giving $\sum_j A_{ij}=1$ per slot, which is
an ordinary row normalization. \citet{zhang2023mesh} identify the pair as a \emph{single}
Sinkhorn iteration, and one iteration does not satisfy both marginals: the second step destroys
the first, so the matrix that multiplies the values is row-stochastic and its columns are
unconstrained. Two things follow, and they cut in opposite directions. The construction reaches
for a column marginal, which is the right object --- but it is uniform and equal to one, where
$c_j$ is learned and free to differ across keys, and the competing set is a bank of slots drawn
each forward pass from one shared learned Gaussian --- deliberately interchangeable, so that no
slot specializes --- rather than the layer's own keys, so what competes is not the data whose
relation is being decided. And because what survives is the row normalization,
Prop.~\ref{prop:conservation} applies to the result.

\textbf{MESH is the closest prior art to MarSea's capacities, on \emph{both} margins, and the
difference has to be stated precisely rather than dismissed.} \citet{zhang2023mesh} improve the
construction by changing the transport \emph{cost}, which is the headline; but their Appendix A
also \emph{learns} the marginals --- and learns both of them: ``\emph{we learn both}
$a = m\cdot\softmaxop(h_a(Z))$ \emph{and} $b = m\cdot\softmaxop(h_b(X))$ \emph{with neural
networks} $h_a,h_b$ \emph{shared across the $m$ slots or $n$ input elements respectively}'', so
that $\sum_i a_i=\sum_j b_j=m$. Read in the orientation above, that is a learned, non-uniform,
content-predicted capacity on the key margin \emph{and} on the query margin, each normalized to a
layer total --- the same objects as $c_j$ with $\sum_j c_j=n_q$ and the row's unit cap. We had
written that both leave the marginals fixed; that was wrong, and it was wrong on both margins,
not one.

\textbf{What is absent is the other half of the object.} A capacity says how much a node may
emit or absorb; it does not say \emph{which} edges carry it, and MESH does not predict that.
Their coupling is an entropy-regularized transport whose solution is \emph{dense} --- the
attention matrix has no exact zeros --- and the strength of the entropy term is a fixed global
hyperparameter, tuned once for the model, not a quantity any head predicts per slot or per input.
So there is no analogue of $\tau_j$ reading a score column, no support to recover, no interval of
Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery} to land in, and nothing that could make Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling}
inapplicable: MESH is a full-support mechanism, so its column precision is pinned at $m_j/n_q$ by
that proposition exactly as row-softmax's is, however well its capacities are learned.
\textbf{That is the sizable difference, and it is a difference in the modelling object rather than
in the parameterization} (Table~\ref{tab:object}).

Two smaller distinctions follow from the same place. \textbf{(i) The field argument.} Their $h_a$
reads the input element \emph{alone} and $h_b$ the slot alone; the capacities are coupled across
participants only through a softmax normalization, and neither reads a shape. Our $\tauK$ reads
the key \emph{together with} its score column and $\nu_j = \|p_{\cdot j}\|_2^{-2}$, a statistic of
the realized column --- the criterion Sec.~\ref{sec:method} states for every head, which
Cor.~\ref{cor:necessity} shows is necessary rather than convenient, and whose consequence
Fig.~\ref{fig:probe}(b) measures. \textbf{(ii) An equality inside a transport, against a cap outside one.} Their marginals
are equality constraints of a doubly stochastic problem, so both margins are pinned to prescribed
totals and Prop.~\ref{prop:conservation} applies with equality; our fan-in side is an
\emph{inequality} cap that never renormalizes, which is what lets a query spend less than a unit
when nothing available deserves it.

\paragraph{Query coupling without a constraint.} Selective Attention
\citep{leviathan2024selective} subtracts an accumulated mask from the attention logits, with
$F_{ij}=\sum_{k\le i-1}S_{kj}$ built from a reused head, so what query $i$ may give key $j$
depends on what \emph{earlier queries} decided about $j$. \textbf{It is therefore not
query-local by Def.~\ref{def:qlocal}, and Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} does not cover it} --- the
only attention mechanism we are aware of for which that is true, and the closest existing relative
of the coupling MarSea introduces. What differs is what the coupling is \emph{for}. $F$ is a
monotone, unbounded penalty accumulated along the prefix: it makes a key progressively less
available, but it enforces nothing. No quantity is constrained, no cardinality is decided, and
the row remains a softmax, so $\sum_j A_{ij}=1$ and Prop.~\ref{prop:conservation} applies
verbatim --- some key still retains a full unit of fan-out however aggressively the mask
accumulates. Our claim is not that queries should be coupled but that the coupling should
\emph{solve a program with a marginal}, which is what turns coupling into a degree bound and a
recovery guarantee. Selective Attention is evidence that the field is reaching for the first
half, and we would rather say so than let a reviewer find it.

\paragraph{Capacity in routing, and the correction we owe expert choice.} Token-choice
mixture-of-experts caps how many tokens an expert accepts and drops the overflow
\citep{lepikhin2021gshard,fedus2022switch}. Expert-choice routing \citep{zhou2022mixture} is the
entry that deserves a mechanism-level answer rather than a motivational one, and an earlier
version of this paper gave the wrong kind. There, each expert independently selects its own
top-$k$ tokens with $k=nc/e$ set by a capacity factor; the selection is over tokens, it therefore
\emph{couples} them, it is a cardinality constraint on the receiving margin, and load balance
holds by construction, so there is no expert overflow to discard --- unlike token choice, where
overflow is dropped. (A token chosen by \emph{no} expert simply skips the layer; that is a
different matter and \citet{zhou2022mixture} do not claim otherwise.) So the coupling and the
cardinality constraint both exist there, and rejecting the comparison on the grounds that
capacity is ``a systems device carrying no claim about the data'' was a category error. What is absent is the part the theory is
about. $k$ is a fixed hyperparameter, uniform across experts and independent of content, where
$\tau_j$ is predicted per key from the key and its column; the selection is a hard top-$k$ rather
than the solution of a constrained program, so there is no allocation to recover, no temperature
to place, and no interval of Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery} to hit; and the adaptive dimension in this
literature is the \emph{token}'s number of experts \citep{yue2024adak}, not the expert's
capacity. Note also what this means for our own framing: Cor.~\ref{cor:capacity} is a statement
about \emph{query-local} schemes, and expert choice is not one, so it is not a counterexample to
anything we prove. Nor does it \emph{escape} the theorem: expert choice is not an attention
mechanism, so it was never inside the theorem's universe. What it shares with Selective Attention
is the \emph{shape} of the move --- decide on the receiving margin, over a set of senders at
once --- and neither turns that move into a guarantee.

\paragraph{Sparse attention, and why we need no relaxation.} Sparsemax
\citep{martins2016softmax} and $\alpha$-entmax \citep{peters2019sparse} yield exact zeros, and
adaptively sparse transformers learn $\alpha$ as a static per-head scalar
\citep{correia2019adaptively}; writing attention as a regularized argmax over a constrained
polytope is the framework of \citet{niculae2017regularized}, generalized by Fenchel--Young losses
\citep{blondel2019fenchel}. MarSea is an instantiation of that framework on the fan-out axis with
content-predicted parameters; \textbf{the variational form is not our contribution}. Our
departure from \citet{correia2019adaptively} is that exclusivity is predicted per key from the
key \emph{together with its score column}, which Cor.~\ref{cor:necessity} argues is necessary
and Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery} argues must read the column, since the admissible interval is a
function of it. The usual route to a differentiable selection is instead to relax a discrete
operator --- a transport relaxation of top-$k$ \citep{xie2020differentiable}, or perturbation of
an arbitrary optimizer \citep{berthet2020perturbed}. We need neither: at $\alpha=2$ the
program's solution is a closed-form truncation (Lem.~\ref{lem:prefix}), differentiable almost
everywhere, with no iteration and no sampling.

\paragraph{Softmax disperses, which is our motivating claim by a cleaner route.}
\citet{velickovic2024softmax} prove that softmax attention cannot remain sharp as the number of
items grows: even for a task as simple as finding the maximum key, any learned circuit must
disperse at test time, and they propose adaptive temperature as an acknowledged ad-hoc remedy.
This is support rather than competition, and it reaches the motivation more cleanly than we do
--- but the remedy is exactly where the two part, and the difference is this paper. Their
temperature is applied to a \emph{row} at inference to sharpen a distribution that keeps full
support; $\tau_j$ is predicted per \emph{key} from that key's column and trained, and the
program it parameterizes produces exact zeros, so what it delivers is a \emph{support} rather
than a sharper point on the simplex. Prop.~\ref{prop:leakage} is the quantitative form of their
qualitative claim on the fan-out margin, and Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling} says why
sharpening alone cannot reach $P_j=1$: precision is pinned by the support, and a temperature does
not change the support.

\paragraph{Representational separations for attention.} \citet{sanford2023representational} is
the reference class for Thm.~\ref{thm:separation}: a positive separation on sparse averaging,
where attention scales logarithmically in the input size and recurrent and feedforward models
polynomially, together with a negative result showing attention itself scales linearly on triple
detection. Their limitations are lower bounds obtained by communication complexity. Ours
separates \emph{within} attention rather than between architectures --- query-local against
query-coupled, at fixed depth and width --- and both the separating object (a constraint rather
than a function) and the method (an exact characterization of an attainable set, with an explicit
witness supplied by Assumption~\ref{ass:richness}) differ. What we take from that line is the
standard it sets for what a separation claim has to exhibit, not a proof technique.

\paragraph{Assignment, agreement, and efficiency.} DETR enforces one-to-one assignment at the
\emph{loss} via Hungarian matching \citep{carion2020end}; capsule routing gates information flow
by agreement between predicted votes and poses \citep{sabour2017dynamic}. MarSea differs from the
capsule family in being non-iterative, requiring no capsule-structured layers, and not
renormalizing the allocation into a distribution over receivers --- $\sum_i \tilde A_{ij}=c_j$
is inherited from the layer, not fixed at one. \emph{Efficiency-motivated sparsifiers} for long context
\citep{xiao2024efficient} reduce the support to save memory; we constrain \emph{degree} for a
correctness guarantee, and Sec.~\ref{sec:experiments} includes matched-sparsity baselines so
that support \emph{size} and \emph{membership} are not confused.

\paragraph{Position bias.} \citet{liu2023lost} is a distinct phenomenon with its own
now-established architectural account: causal masking and residual connections produce a U-shaped
influence profile over \emph{positions} already at initialization, independently of positional
encodings \citep{chowdhury2026birth,wu2025emergence,herasimchyk2026residual}. We make no claim
about that axis; see Sec.~\ref{sec:apps} and App.~\ref{app:limitations}.

\section{Limitations, continued}
\label{app:limitations}

\paragraph{The four stated in Sec.~\ref{sec:experiments}, in full.} \textbf{Single-layer scope:}
Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} bounds what one attention operation can guarantee, not the asymptotic
expressivity of a deep query-local stack. \textbf{No output-error guarantee:}
Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery} concerns the \emph{support} of the allocation and does not bound
$\|O_i - O_i^\star\|$ without further assumptions on the values, and
the margin-conditioned output bounds available for pairwise gating do not transfer, since under
column coupling $A_{ij}$ is not a function of the pair alone. \textbf{Everything is scoped to the
active set:} conditional activation is part of the mechanism, so every result above concerns
the relation, while pairs outside it inherit standard attention's precision ceiling; worse,
an empty relation everywhere is a degenerate optimum \emph{easier} to reach than collapse in
$\tau_j$, and we have a diagnostic for it rather than a training scheme that avoids it.
\textbf{Fidelity needs ground truth:} precision against a relation nobody annotated is not a
number, and we do not report one.

\paragraph{The positional account, and why it is a different claim.} Causal masking and residual
connections produce a U-shaped influence profile over \emph{positions}, and the literature agrees
it is architectural rather than learned while differing on what else is required.
\citet{chowdhury2026birth} derives it at initialization independently of positional encodings;
\citet{wu2025emergence} obtain it from the causal mask \emph{competing with} relative positional
encodings such as decay masks and RoPE, so encodings are part of the mechanism rather than
factored out; \citet{herasimchyk2026residual} treat causal masking, the residual stream,
positional encodings and content as four interacting forces. We do not need to adjudicate that,
and we do not: what all three supply is a statement about
\emph{where} a gold item sits and is orthogonal to ours, which is about \emph{how many}
candidates accompany it: the two would predict the same aggregate degradation for different
reasons, which is why E3 holds gold position fixed while sweeping $n$ and reports the
position-marginal profile alongside. A gap that vanishes once position is controlled would mean
we are measuring the U-shape and not the candidate count, and we would report that. We do not
claim to explain the U-shape and MarSea is not a remedy for it.

\paragraph{The failure-path tally, and what it omits.} On the column, recall can fail from
$\tau_j$ above the recovery interval, from $\theta_i$ too high, and from $K<m_j$ at a leaf,
against a single precision path ($\tau_j$ below the interval); on the row, recall can fail from
$\theta_i$ too high and by inheriting a Stage-1 false negative, against one precision path
($\theta_i$ too low). That $3\!:\!1$ and $2\!:\!1$ counts only the two programs. Two further
\emph{precision} paths enter once the rest of the mechanism is in scope: a key outside a
row's relation contributes full-support attention to that row, and the frozen-prefix variant over-admits
(Prop.~\ref{prop:prefix}(iii)). Both are precision-side, so they do not disturb the conclusion
that recall is the fragile side; they do mean the ratio should be read as a lower bound on the
asymmetry rather than an exact count.

\paragraph{Why independent per-target selection over-commits a source.} This is the content of
Prop.~\ref{prop:scope}(ii), and it is worth stating in words. If $M$ targets are alike enough
with respect to one source that the same source wins at each of them, then a query-local scheme
assigns it to all $M$ --- no row can know that another row has already claimed it. The failure
is not that the scheme cannot select; it is that it cannot decline to be selected.

\paragraph{Thm.~\ref{thm:separation}'s hypotheses, in self-attention.} The theorem fixes
$\{k_j\}$ while each $x_i$ ranges over $\mathcal{D}_i$, and it is fair to ask whether the two are
jointly satisfiable when $q_i$ and $k_i$ are projections of one token. \textbf{Fixing the keys
does not by itself collapse $\mathcal{D}_i$.} Write $q_i=W_q u_i$, $k_i=W_k u_i$ with
$W_q,W_k:\mathbb{R}^d\to\mathbb{R}^{d_h}$ and $u_i$ the vector the projections read. For $W_k$ of
full rank, fixing every key confines $u_i$ to $u_i+\ker W_k$, of dimension $d-d_h$; and unless
$\operatorname{row}(W_q)\subseteq\operatorname{row}(W_k)$, in which case $W_q$ vanishes on
$\ker W_k$ and queries are fixed outright, the restriction $W_q|_{\ker W_k}$ has rank
$\min(d_h,\,d-d_h)$. So $q_i$ still moves in a $d_h$-dimensional set once $d\ge 2d_h$, and
under pre-LN, where $u_i$ additionally lies on a $(d{-}2)$-dimensional manifold, once
$d\ge 2d_h+2$ --- three or more heads at $d=Hd_h$. Two honest caveats: the rank statements are
generic rather than universal, and trained weights are not a generic draw, so the row-space
degeneracy is measurable and unmeasured; and rank is not conditioning --- a small
$\sigma_{\min}(W_q|_{\ker W_k})$ makes the required displacement large, which the pre-LN
constraint bounds. \textbf{What this establishes is the negative claim, not a positive one}: the
key constraint alone does not make Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} vacuous. Whether the tuple
$(u_1,\dots,u_{n_q})$ ranges over a \emph{product} is a separate question, and it is the one
Assumption~\ref{ass:richness} answers --- so the division of labour is that
Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} supplies the \emph{shape} of what a query-local scheme attains, on
whatever product domain it is given, and Assumption~\ref{ass:richness} with
Cor.~\ref{cor:capacity} supplies the \emph{bite}. \textbf{S0 measures the second}, which is the
half a shrunken domain can actually threaten.

\paragraph{Why the margins do not share a head.} A single exclusivity head would have to accept
$(k_j,s_{\cdot j}) \in \mathbb{R}^d\times\mathbb{R}^{n_q}$ and $(q_i,\tilde A_{i\cdot}) \in
\mathbb{R}^d\times\mathbb{R}^{n_k}$ --- spaces of different dimension that coincide only in
self-attention with $n_q=n_k$, and never in cross-attention or in the causal form of
Sec.~\ref{sec:method-causal}, where the column runs over $Q_j(t)$ and the row over the visible
keys. Even where a shared head is definable it would be aimed at two targets at once:
$\tauK$ must land in Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery}'s interval, whose endpoints are functions of the
score column, while $\tauQ$ must place the dual $\theta_i$ in Prop.~\ref{prop:graded}(ii)'s,
whose endpoints are order statistics of the incoming allocation. The relation is one object by
construction, so there is no pair of gates to tie or untie; what E9 ablates on that side is
whether $e_{ij}$ reads the pair or the key alone. E9 ablates the tying of $\tauK$ to $\tauQ$
in self-attention, where it is definable, and we predict it costs; the cost is what makes the
separation mechanism rather than bookkeeping.
\textbf{Why the cap follows the column program, and why the margins are not fused.} Applied
beforehand the cap would merely shift scores that the column program would normalize away; and
coupling both margins into one equality-budgeted program would be a capacitated transport
problem needing Sinkhorn-style alternation, where two one-sided constraints in turn need a single
sort each.
\textbf{Stage 2 stands alone, and that bounds what it can do.} \eqref{eq:rowprog} is defined for
any non-negative row, which is what makes the relation of Sec.~\ref{sec:method-gate} coherent: a row
generally mixes entries re-shaped by column relations with entries left to standard attention, and
the cap applies to the mixture. \textbf{What the cap does there is bound the total, not
re-commensurate the two scales.} Eq.~\eqref{eq:rowprog} applies one offset and one gain, both
uniform across $j$, and a group-blind affine map cannot correct a group-dependent scale
mismatch: it thresholds both groups at the same absolute level, so whichever group carries the
smaller magnitudes is eliminated first irrespective of relevance. We treat this as an
approximation the mixture forces rather than a repair, and it is why Cor.~\ref{cor:joint} is
stated for consistent ground truths on rows whose relation is the whole row.
Every row-margin guarantee inherits the standalone property, their proofs using only
that truncation is coordinatewise, monotone and fixes zero --- properties the gain $\tau_i>0$
preserves, so each result is stated in terms of $\theta_i$, a threshold on $\tilde A_{i\cdot}$.
The converse is the limitation: applied to an ordinary attention row, \eqref{eq:rowprog} is a
function of that query's own inputs and the keys, hence query-local by Def.~\ref{def:qlocal}, so
it bounds that row's fan-in --- which needs only the cap --- while by Thm.~\ref{thm:separation}
leaving fan-out entirely untouched.
A row-side deployment of MarSea is therefore a real mechanism with a real guarantee, but it is not
the paper's central claim, which needs Stage 1.
\textbf{Row mass is bounded above by the row's own softmax mass, and not below.}
$\sum_jA_{ij}\le\sum_jA^{\mathrm{sm}}_{ij}$ (Prop.~\ref{prop:twostep}(iii)), which is one unit in
exact arithmetic, so no query ever absorbs more than the unit softmax spends --- but a query whose relation is slack, or whose $\tau_i<1$ scales its relation
down, spends \emph{less}. That is deliberate (it is what lets a query decline to be explained by the keys
available) and it is a real difference from softmax, which spends exactly one unit always: output
magnitudes now vary across queries within a layer, and downstream normalization sees a wider
range than it would otherwise. \textbf{What the cap bounds unconditionally is fan-in
\emph{mass}.} It bounds fan-in \emph{degree} only where it binds: on a slack row $\theta_i=0$ and
$\supp(A_{i\cdot})=\supp(\tilde A_{i\cdot})$ exactly, so the row's degree is whatever Stage 1 and
the relation left it, and Stage 2 is a positive scalar gain. The degree constraint on this margin is
therefore conditional on $\tau_i>1$ (a floor above step 1's) in a way the column's is not, and E8 reports the
fraction of rows on which the cap binds beside the coverage rates.
\textbf{The admission floor is a dual, not a knob, and its range is bounded.} $\theta_i$ is
determined by $(\tilde A_{i\cdot},\tau_i)$: as $\tau_i$ ranges over $(0,\infty)$ it traverses
$[0,\max_j\tilde A_{ij})$ continuously and strictly increasingly, and $\theta_i=0$ is
\emph{forced} on any row arriving within budget. Two consequences. Every $\theta_i$-conditioned
statement above should be read as quantifying over that attainable range rather than over an
arbitrary floor; and Prop.~\ref{prop:graded}(ii)'s row interval can be \emph{unreachable} at
every admissible $\tau_i$ --- in particular a row whose weakest true source is its largest entry
can never shed it, so $R_i\ge1/|K_i|$ unconditionally. Where a predicted cap would have made row
exactness a matter of parameter choice, the unit cap makes it partly a matter of reachability,
and E7 reports attainability rather than only the sign of the margin.
\textbf{Truncation rescales the survivors.} The soft form $\tau_i[\tilde A_{ij}-\theta_i]_+$
subtracts $\theta_i$ from every edge it keeps and then applies a common gain, so relative
magnitudes within a row are not preserved even where the support is exact; a hard variant
preserves them at the cost of differentiability at the threshold, and we ablate both. \textbf{The two margins are
constrained in different currencies}, a mass budget and an admission floor. The asymmetry is what
preserves a closed-form, non-iterative solution, but a genuinely two-sided budgeted formulation
--- a capacitated transport problem --- remains open. \textbf{$\alpha=2$ specificity.}
Monotone exclusivity and Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery} are proved for sparsemax, which is the
setting we deploy; monotonicity is numerically consistent at $\alpha\in\{1.2,1.5,1.8,3\}$ but
unproven there. \textbf{Not invariant to the query set.} The program couples queries, so MarSea is
permutation-equivariant but not invariant to insertion or deletion of queries. \textbf{The causal
form interacts with the key--value cache.} $\psi_j$ is a function of the visible column, so
admitting a new query alters previously computed entries, whereas ordinary causal attention fixes
$A_{ij}$ once computed. Prop.~\ref{prop:prefix} bounds the damage in the direction that matters
and blocking holds the cost at $O(n_kB\log B)$ per step, but the
two emission policies are a real choice --- seal-at-boundary is exact at up to $B$ tokens of
latency, frozen-prefix is fully incremental and over-admits --- and we report
both rather than claim one dominates. Relatedly, $\tau_j$ reads $\nu_j$, which is a statistic of $p_{\cdot j}$ and so is unavailable
from the block being solved; it is taken from the previous block of the same key (or the previous
layer in an encoder), and is initialised at $1$, the value of a singleton support, on the first.
\textbf{Degeneracy.} If $\tau_j$ collapses to a constant across keys, or $\tau_i$ to a constant
across queries, the corresponding margin loses all content dependence --- the column reducing to
a fixed-temperature entmax on the relation. \textbf{With the capacity inherited this is cheaper
than it was}: one parameter per margin carries the whole claim. Two collapses must be told
apart. An empty relation \emph{is} standard attention entrywise (Prop.~\ref{prop:twostep}(vi))
and is the degenerate optimum easiest to reach; $\tau_j\to0$ on a non-empty relation is
\emph{not} --- it spreads the inherited quota uniformly over the relation
(Lem.~\ref{lem:prefix}: at $\tau_j\to0$ every prefix has $G<1$, so the support is the whole relation and the entries tend to $\bar c_j/|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$), a column of the wrong shape, and shows up as a fan-out degree of
$|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ with precision $m_j/|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ on a column the mechanism
did touch. All three are directly measurable as across-key and across-query variances and are
reported. A subtler variant is a head that ignores its field argument, behaving as
$\tauK(k_j,\cdot)$; it is invisible to those variances, which is why E9 ablates the field
argument directly rather than inferring it.

\section{Selective exclusivity: scoping both programs to a relation}
\label{app:selective}

Both programs of Sec.~\ref{sec:method} are defined on an \emph{index set}, and nothing in either
requires that set to be a whole column or a whole row. This appendix states the generalization
that follows, says which results survive it and which do not, and gives our reason for thinking
it is the direction this mechanism goes. \textbf{Since the fifth revision it \emph{is} the
mechanism}: Sec.~\ref{sec:method} is stated on the relation, and this appendix keeps the
derivation and the costs, and governs how Assumption~\ref{ass:margin} should be read as the
candidate set grows.

\paragraph{The generalization.} A key is rarely exclusive over everything. A left-turn arrow
governs the left-turn lanes in its direction and nothing else: it is not exclusive over the
through lanes, and not exclusive over how many vehicles are waiting in them. The same holds on
the query margin --- a lane is exclusive over the signals that govern \emph{it}, not over every
signal at the intersection. Let $\mathcal{E}\subseteq[n_q]\times[n_k]$ be an \textbf{exclusive
subgraph}, with slices written in the paper's own convention,
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j} := \{i : (i,j)\in\mathcal{E}\},
\qquad
\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot} := \{j : (i,j)\in\mathcal{E}\},
\label{eq:subgraph}
\end{equation}
and run Stage 1 \eqref{eq:program} on $\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}$ and Stage 2 \eqref{eq:rowprog} on
$\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}$, pairs outside $\mathcal{E}$ keeping ordinary attention. We call this
\textbf{selective exclusivity}. Two structural remarks. First, conditional activation
(Sec.~\ref{sec:method-gate}) is the special case
$\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}\in\{\emptyset,[n_q]\}$, and the query gate the case
$\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}\in\{\emptyset,[n_k]\}$: both gates become degenerate masks rather than
separate components, and the five heads of App.~\ref{app:notation} become three, at the cost of
whatever parameterizes the mask. Second, both
margins must be slices of \emph{one} object. Independent per-margin masks would let Stage 2 act
on entries of $\tilde A_{i\cdot}$ that Stage 1 never constrained, which puts
Prop.~\ref{prop:stagecomp} and the consistency hypothesis of Cor.~\ref{cor:joint} back in
question; we do not consider them.

\paragraph{What transfers.} Table~\ref{tab:transfer} is the accounting. The pattern is that
results which are statements about a sparsemax and a truncation \emph{over an index set} carry
over with the index set replaced, while results that count the set do not.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Restriction of both programs to an exclusive subgraph, statement by statement. Rows in
the last group are the ones a development of this idea would owe a proof.}
\label{tab:transfer}
\scriptsize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Result} & \textbf{Under restriction} & \textbf{Because} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{@{}l}{\emph{Verbatim, with $[n_q]\!\to\!\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}$ and $[n_k]\!\to\!\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}$}} \\
Lem.~\ref{lem:prefix} & unchanged & a property of sparsemax on a set \\
Monotone exclusivity & $\tau\to0^+$ endpoint reads $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ & it names the set size \\
Thm.~\ref{thm:separation}, separation & unchanged & the product is over the index set \\
Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery} and its endpoints & unchanged & $W_j,m_j,\delta_j$ carry no set size \\
Prop.~\ref{prop:printerval}, \ref{prop:graded}, \ref{prop:tournament} & unchanged & same \\
Prop.~\ref{prop:stagecomp}, composition & unchanged & one mask $\Rightarrow$ both stages agree \\
Cor.~\ref{cor:necessity}, necessity & unchanged & a comparison of intervals \\
Prop.~\ref{prop:prefix}, causal form & unchanged \emph{if} $\mathcal{E}$ is arrival-sealed & growth must add, never revoke \\
\midrule
