Prop.~\ref{prop:leakage}, dilution & $n_q\to|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ \emph{at matched domain} & it describes a comparator \\
Cor.~\ref{cor:joint}, joint attainment & consistency induced by $\mathcal{E}$ if $T_j\subseteq\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}$ & one object, not two ground truths \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{@{}l}{\emph{Genuinely different, and owed a proof}} \\
Prop.~\ref{prop:conservation}, conservation & $n_q/n_k\to\mu/n_k$, $\mu$ the in-relation mass & off-$\mathcal{E}$ mass escapes it \\
The unit row cap & bounds in-relation mass only & the cap sees only $\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}$ \\
Budget normalization $\sum_j c_j=n_q$ & \textbf{derived, not imposed} & step 1 matches standard attention \\
Prop.~\ref{prop:realizability}, realizability & partition of the covered set only & construction must be re-run \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Why: $n_q$ enters the theory in exactly one place.} Of the three quantities fixing
Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery}'s interval, $W_j$ and $m_j$ are functions of the target set alone; only
$\delta_j$ looks outside it, and it looks by taking a \emph{max over the complement}. A max over a
growing pool is non-decreasing in the pool size, so $\delta_j$ is \emph{non-increasing in $n_q$
whatever the score law}, and it converges to
$\min_{i\in T_j}s_{ij}-\operatorname*{ess\,sup}(\text{non-target law})$ --- which is negative
whenever a non-target can outrank the weakest target with positive probability. Past that point
Assumption~\ref{ass:margin} fails outright, not because the scoring function is poor but because
the assumption is a statement about a set that keeps growing.

\textbf{We state it this way rather than as a divergence, and the reason is our own model of the
representation.} If the non-target law has unbounded support above --- $\mathcal{N}(\mu_0,
\sigma^2)$, say --- then $\delta_j\to-\infty$ at rate $\sqrt{\ln n_q}$, since the maximum is
$\mu_0+\sigma\sqrt{2\ln(n_q-m_j)}(1+o(1))$. But under pre-LN the vector each projection reads
lies on a bounded manifold (App.~\ref{app:limitations}), so $q_i\!\cdot\!k_j/\sqrt d$ is
bounded and the scores are not Gaussian in the tail: the max converges \emph{up to} an essential
supremum instead of diverging. The monotone-convergence form above is what survives that, it is
all the argument needs, and it is not removed by training, which moves the law but not the
monotonicity. Past that point every result in Sec.~\ref{sec:recovery} is
vacuous rather than merely weak. Restricted to $\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}$, the max is over a set
whose size does not grow with the context, and $\delta_j$ becomes $n_q$-free by construction.
Table~\ref{tab:margin} measures both, with a scoring function that already separates non-targets
by two standard deviations.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Margin and exact recovery against candidate count, at $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|=8$,
$m_j=3$, non-targets outside the scope drawn $\mathcal{N}(-2,1)$; $3000$ columns per row,
$\tau_j$ taken inside the scope's interval. The scope margin is identical across rows by
construction; the column margin is not. \textbf{Both $\delta_j$ columns are means, and the
recovery columns are not a function of them}, which is the honest way to read the table: recovery
is per column, and Assumption~\ref{ass:margin} fails on a growing \emph{fraction} of columns
rather than switching off at a threshold. Already at $n_q=32$, where the mean margin is still
positive, $26.5\%$ of columns have $\delta_j\le0$ and can be recovered at no temperature; at
$n_q=128$, where the mean has gone negative, $24.5\%$ still satisfy the assumption, which is what
