\paragraph{What this settles about the comparison with softmax, and what it does not.}
Prop.~\ref{prop:leakage} sets a $\log n_q$ requirement against one with ``no $n_q$ in it at
all''. On the unrestricted mechanism that contrast is stated more strongly than it should be:
$n_q$ survives inside $\delta_j$ on \emph{both} sides, so the honest residual is the logarithmic
factor and not the presence or absence of $n$. Under restriction there are two gains and they
deserve separate credit. \textbf{(i)} The $\log|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ factor belongs to the
\emph{program} --- a terminated tail rather than an exponential one --- and is unaffected by the
mask. \textbf{(ii)} The reduction of the domain from $n_q$ to $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ belongs
to the \emph{mask}, and a masked softmax would enjoy it equally. What restriction contributes
beyond either is that Assumption~\ref{ass:margin} stops being asymptotically vacuous, so (i)
remains a claim about something. We regard this as the strongest argument for the generalization
and the correct reading of Prop.~\ref{prop:leakage} without it.

\paragraph{What it costs, and what it no longer costs.} An earlier version of this appendix
listed four costs of restriction. \textbf{The two-step form of Sec.~\ref{sec:method} retires two
of them and improves on a third}, because step 1 fixes the complement rather than leaving it free.
We set out the accounting rather than quietly dropping it.

\textbf{(a) The separation theorem's force is parameterized by $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$, and
vanishes at $1$ --- this one survives.} Cardinality on a singleton \emph{is} product-form, so a
query-local scheme enforces it by clipping and Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} says nothing; the
replication pair of Assumption~\ref{ass:richness} must also be found inside the set, giving
$M\le|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$. A learned relation can therefore shrink its way out of the theorem.
Worse, the degeneracy is invisible to the \emph{collapse variances} of
App.~\ref{app:limitations}: at $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|=1$ the mechanism is still masking and
$\tau_j$ still varies across keys while every constraint has quietly become product-form. It is
not undetectable --- every column then has $k^\star=1$ and $\nu_j=1$, and
$T_j\subseteq\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}$ forces $m_j\le1$, so E8's realized-$k^\star$ report shows it
--- and any deployment must report the $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ \emph{distribution}, not its
mean, from the first epoch, and carry $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|\ge2$ wherever
Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} is invoked. It also adds an experimental axis: the E2 gap should shrink
as $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|\to1$, a pre-committed falsification of the same shape as E4.

\textbf{(b) The conservation obstruction was said to weaken --- retired.} The worry was that a
scheme could evade Prop.~\ref{prop:conservation} by parking mass off $\mathcal{E}$. Under
\eqref{eq:step1} the off-relation entries are whatever an unmodified layer allocates: they are
fixed by step 1 and untouched by step 2 (Prop.~\ref{prop:twostep}(iv)), so they are not a free
parameter and there is nothing to park. Prop.~\ref{prop:conservation} is in any case a statement
about row-normalized \emph{baselines}, and restriction changes nothing any baseline does.

\textbf{(c) The unit row cap was said to stop covering the row --- retired.}
\eqref{eq:rowstep1} caps the \emph{whole} row at the unit it already carries, before any
restriction is applied, and step 2 conserves the relation's quota within it, so the row total
never exceeds its own softmax mass and the complement keeps its step-1 value. The earlier version left the complement free and the row total
could approach two; that is no longer the construction.

\textbf{(d) The capacity anchor was said to have no mask-independent analogue --- improved on.}
$\sum_j c_j=n_q$ is no longer imposed at all: $c_j$ is the mass an unmodified layer gives the key,
so the identity is a consequence (Prop.~\ref{prop:twostep}(ii)) and holds at every relation. What
this costs instead is the \emph{capacity half} of the modelling object --- the mechanism no longer
predicts how much a key emits into its relation, only how many members receive it. That is stated
in Sec.~\ref{sec:method-fanout} and in Cor.~\ref{cor:capacity}, and it is why E1 measures degree.

\textbf{(e) A new cost, and the one we would press on if we were reviewing.} With the capacity
inherited, each margin has a single parameter carrying its whole claim, where a predicted budget
would have been a second and independent constraint. Collapse is correspondingly cheaper: where previously $c_j$, $\tau_j$ and $\tau_i$ had to
degenerate together, now either the relation emptying (standard attention,
Prop.~\ref{prop:twostep}(vi)) or $\tau_j\to0$ on a non-empty relation (the inherited quota
spread uniformly over it --- \emph{not} standard attention, and a column re-shaped to the wrong
shape) removes the fan-out claim on its own. E8 reports the $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$
distribution, the across-key variance of $\tau_j$ and the realized $k^\star$ distribution from
epoch one for this reason, and the implementation initialises the relation near-empty so that
training starts at standard attention rather than at a uniformly re-shaped one.

\paragraph{How the mask must be parameterized, and why that is not a retreat.} A free per-edge
binary mask is $n_q n_k$ discrete decisions, is unidentifiable, and is the strong form of exactly
what App.~\ref{app:scaling} declines for a learned per-block $K$. The right object is a typed or
low-rank compatibility that \emph{induces} the mask,
$e_{ij}=\mathbf{1}[\langle u_\phi(k_j),v_\phi(q_i)\rangle>0]$ at rank $r$, costing
$O((n_q{+}n_k)r)$ and reading the traffic example literally: a class of key against a class of
query. This has a consequence for Sec.~\ref{sec:method-causal}. Because $e_{ij}$ depends on the
pair alone, membership decided when a query arrives is never revoked, $\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}(t)$
is nested increasing, and Prop.~\ref{prop:prefix} goes through unchanged; a mask that could
reconsider does not, and in simulation breaks permanence of exclusion on $90.7\%$ of prefixes. A
pairwise mask is thus \emph{required} by the causal form rather than merely tolerated.

That deserves saying plainly, because it looks at first like a concession: the paper's argument
is that a pairwise decision cannot govern a multivariate relation, and here membership is decided
pairwise. The two are compatible, and the distinction is the point.
\emph{Whether this key and this query stand in the relation} is a question about the pair.
\emph{Which of the competing keys wins this query, given the others} is not, and that is what
Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} is about. Pairwise decides who is in the room; the joint program
decides who wins; the mask restricts a \emph{domain} while the program does the
\emph{constraining}. This also clarifies rather than weakens the case for field-reading heads:
the field argument was always needed by $\tauK$ and $\tauQ$, whose job is to hit the interval of
Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery}, and never by the membership decision.

\paragraph{Where it helps least.} In the evidence-selection setting of Sec.~\ref{sec:apps},
$\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}$ for an answer query is plausibly the entire retrieved candidate set, since
the passages are candidates by construction, and a learned relation that admits them all changes
nothing in E2 or E3 relative to the unrestricted form. Its natural settings are those with typed
structure --- a pronoun is exclusive over \emph{mentions} and not over every token; a variable
binding over the \emph{references} to that variable. Fidelity measurement on the column needs
$T_j$ with several members, which typed structure supplies directly and multi-hop evidence
supplies through the bridge entity's later mentions (App.~\ref{app:expprotocol}, E7).

\paragraph{Numerical check of the transfer.} The claims of the first group are checked directly,
by running both programs on a scope rather than a margin, in the same harness that produces
Table~\ref{tab:verification}; Table~\ref{tab:transferchecks} gives the counts. The margin block is
Table~\ref{tab:margin}. Two of these are checks that something \emph{fails}: the
$n_q/n_k$ bound of Prop.~\ref{prop:conservation} is escaped on every trial once mass may sit off
$\mathcal{E}$, and permanence of exclusion breaks on $90.7\%$ of prefixes if the mask is allowed
to reconsider. The first is weaker than it looks and we mark it so: the generator draws the mask
density from $[0.02,0.5]$, so the covered mass cannot reach $n_q/n_k$ and the escape is arranged
rather than discovered --- what the row shows is that the bound is not automatic under
restriction, not how far it falls. As everywhere in App.~\ref{app:numerics}, these establish that
the statements are true of the objects the program constructs; they are not evidence about
trained networks, and no mask here is learned.

