\paragraph{How large must $K$ be?} Prop.~\ref{prop:tournament}(i) makes hierarchical selection
exact whenever $K\ge k^\star$, so the practical question is how large $k^\star$ gets. Over $4000$
columns per regime, with $n_q$ drawn from $\{64,\dots,2048\}$ and scores $\mathcal{N}(0,2^2)$
scaled by $\tau$: when the column is \emph{exclusive} ($\tau\in[0.5,6]$) the median is $1$, the
$99$th percentile $5$ and the maximum $8$; in a \emph{mixed} regime ($\tau\in[0.1,20]$) the
maximum is $14$; and on deliberately \emph{broad} columns ($\tau\in[0.02,1]$) the median is $4$,
the $99$th percentile $35$ and the maximum $74$. An earlier version of this work reported a single
figure and concluded that a fixed $K$ in the tens is universally safe; that was too strong. The
corrected statement is more useful, because it makes the choice of $K$ an empirical question
about the deployed model rather than a constant asserted in advance. This is why E8 reports the
realized $k^\star$ distribution jointly with $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ rather than either alone: the
relation has the information needed to keep $k^\star$ small (App.~\ref{app:scaling}), and that
report is what tells us whether it used it.

\paragraph{Why some rows need few trials, and why the counts are not comparable.}
Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling} is a deterministic identity rather than a statement that
could fail on a hard draw, so it is checked on a small grid of $(n_q,n_k,m_j,|K_i|)$ spanning
four orders of magnitude rather than on a large sample; a larger count would add no risk to it.
That reasoning generalizes, which is why the tables carry daggers rather than a single total:
\textbf{a large denominator on a daggered row means the loop ran many times, not that the claim
survived many chances to fail.} The counts are also in different units of work ---
Prop.~\ref{prop:prefix} under a nested scope contributes $131{,}180$ because it counts every
prefix timestep of every sequence, where a column swept at four temperatures contributes four ---
so the rows should be read individually and not summed. The blocks with large \emph{informative}
counts are those whose hypotheses interact.

\paragraph{The row checks and $\tau_i$.} Every row-margin claim quantifies over the admission
floor $\theta_i$, and the harness sweeps $\theta_i$ to match. This is legitimate, but not because
$\theta_i$ is free: it is the dual of the unit cap, and $\tau_i\mapsto\theta_i$ is a strictly
increasing bijection onto $[0,\max_j\tilde A_{ij})$, so sweeping $\theta_i$ over that range is
\emph{equivalent} to sweeping $\tau_i$ over $(0,\infty)$. What the gain $\tau_i>0$ then
contributes is a positive common factor, which moves no support and changes no precision or
recall, so the support-level counts below are $\tau_i$-free and the shipped harness needs no
$\tau_i$. Note what this does \emph{not} cover: the harness tests the row claims at a given
floor, never whether the head's realized $\tau_i$ puts the floor where the row needs it, which is
E7.

\paragraph{What the daggered rows do and do not test, and why we mark them.} A check can only
fail if the generator can produce an input on which the conclusion is false, and for a
\emph{constructive} proposition it often cannot. Prop.~\ref{prop:stagecomp}'s three rows are the
clearest case: the program forms $A_{ij}=[\tilde A_{ij}-\theta_i]_+$ from a non-negative
$\tilde A$, which \emph{makes} $S^{(2)}\subseteq S^{(1)}$, and (i), (ii) and (iv) are corollaries
of that inclusion evaluated on the same columns. Prop.~\ref{prop:graded}(ii)'s rows plant the
threshold strictly between the true and false sources, and its recall row sorts the row before
naming the top entries as the true ones, so ``weakest-first'' is guaranteed by the sort.
Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling}'s rows evaluate $m_j/n_q = m_j/n_q$ on a full support.
Prop.~\ref{prop:conservation}'s restricted row is $\max\ge\mathrm{mean}$ over $n_k$ non-negative
numbers. \textbf{These rows confirm that the algebra in the proof is the algebra in the
statement, which is worth having and is how two sign errors were caught; they are not independent
evidence that the propositions are true, because the generator cannot produce a counterexample.}
Rebuilding them to sample inputs that could violate the conclusion --- unsorted sources, thresholds
that may land inside a group --- is the natural next version of this harness and we have not done
it.

The undaggered rows are the ones that could have failed and did not. Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery}'s
three rows place $\tau_j$ on both sides of two independently computed endpoints;
Prop.~\ref{prop:tournament}(ii) reports fidelity at $K<m_j$, where the claim is an inequality
that a bad bound would break; Cor.~\ref{cor:joint} runs the full pipeline and asks for four
quantities at once; Prop.~\ref{prop:prefix} sweeps a growing prefix. Those are where a genuine
error would have surfaced, and one did.

\paragraph{What none of it establishes.} These are not evidence about real data: every score
configuration is synthetic and every ground-truth relation planted. How wide the recovery interval is on a trained network is E7, not
anything in this appendix. The joint-attainment row draws the target sets $\{T_j\}$ as a partition
of the queries so that $K_i=\{j: i\in T_j\}$; that is precisely the consistency hypothesis
Cor.~\ref{cor:joint} now states, and without it ``both margins at once'' has no well-defined
ground truth to check against.

\section{Experimental protocol in full}
\label{app:expprotocol}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Estimated single-GPU cost. H100-hours, $\pm50\%$ per item; the per-stage figures are
run estimates and the $1.5\times$ debugging allowance is applied once, at the bottom, so the
column sums as printed. Runs within a stage parallelize across a node.}
\label{tab:compute}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Stage} & \textbf{Contents; gate and pivot} & \textbf{H100-h} \\
\midrule
S0 & E1 on trained-backbone scores. \emph{Gate}: a replication pair, $M>m_j$ at stated $a$. & 1 \\
S1 & Mechanism correctness: identities, hierarchical-vs-flat agreement, relation logging, & 15 \\
   & end-to-end training. \emph{Gate}: identities to tolerance; relation coverage non-zero. & \\
\textbf{S2} & \textbf{E3 $n$-sweep with both controls; E2 and E4 on RULER.} \emph{Gate}: precision & \textbf{60} \\
   & flat in $n$ on $\delta_j>0$ columns; hit rate not falling in $m$. \emph{If not}: report the & \\
   & training-effect claim and rescope the paper. & \\
S3 & E5 MuSiQue/HotpotQA; E7 fidelity; E8 diagnostics. & 68 \\
S4 & E6 IHEval depth stratification; E9 ablations. & 80 \\
\midrule
\midrule
Subtotal & (adaptation fine-tunes included in S2--S4) & 224 \\
\textbf{Total} & \textbf{with the $1.5\times$ debugging allowance} & \textbf{$\approx$336} \\
