\paragraph{Staging.} Table~\ref{tab:compute} gives the five stages, their gates
and the pivot taken if a gate fails. S0 and S1 together cost $16$ H100-hours and can invalidate
either the applicability of Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} (no replication pair on real score columns)
or the correctness of the implementation, before any expensive stage is entered. Figures carry
$\pm50\%$ per item; the stages sum to $224$ and the $1.5\times$ debugging allowance is applied
once to that subtotal.

\paragraph{E1, construction.} \textbf{The probe measures degree, not mass}, because under
\eqref{eq:step1} the mass a key emits into its relation is inherited and a mass sweep would show
MarSea tracking softmax by construction. Panel (a): the true relation size is held at $m_j=4$ and
the candidate set $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ takes the values $4,8,\dots,256$; the $m_j$ members
score $1+U(0,1)$ and the distractors $0.9\cdot U(0,1)$, so Assumption~\ref{ass:margin} holds with
$\delta_j\ge0.1$ at every size --- \emph{by construction}, which is the point of a possibility
result and is not what App.~\ref{app:selective} says happens on an unrestricted column. MarSea's
$\tau_j$ is placed inside Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery}'s interval computed from the planted set, at
$\mathrm{lo}+\tfrac12(\min(\mathrm{hi},20\,\mathrm{lo})-\mathrm{lo})$; \textbf{it is an oracle
temperature}, and MarSea's curve is therefore Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery}'s \emph{iff} drawn rather
than a measurement of a head --- what the figure shows is the shape of the guarantee, and the
comparator's side is Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling}, also an identity. The comparator's
support is $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ and its precision $m_j/|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ at every
temperature and scoring function, so it is drawn analytically rather than sampled; MESH and every
dense scheme lie on the same line. Twelve seeds vary the scores and are inert on both curves,
which is what an identity looks like. Panel (b): six keys at $n_q=63$ with true sizes
$1,2,4,8,16,32$; three of the $72$ columns drawn violated Assumption~\ref{ass:margin} on the
first draw (their interval is not an interval and the midpoint temperature is negative) and were
re-clipped rather than reported, and the script prints how many. Everything E1 establishes is
already in Table~\ref{tab:verification}'s rows; its value is the picture, and S0 is the
measurement.

\paragraph{E4, E5 and E6 in full.} \textbf{E4} repeats E2 at $m=1$ on RULER S-NIAH, the regime
Prop.~\ref{prop:scope}(i) concedes to query-local schemes. \emph{Prediction}: a gap near zero. A
\emph{large} gap here is the interesting negative --- it would mean the benefit is not coming
from multivariate structure, and E2's reading would need revision. \textbf{E5} externalises to
MuSiQue ($m\in\{2,3,4\}$ by hop) and HotpotQA-distractor; its $m$-stratification is confounded
with difficulty by construction, so it corroborates E2 and never replaces it. \textbf{E6} tests
the ranked half of Sec.~\ref{sec:intro} on IHEval, stratifying by the number of conflicting
instruction tiers and reporting accuracy \emph{as a function of depth}, the slope rather than the
pooled mean. \emph{Prediction}: at depth one --- two sources, one overriding --- MarSea and a
well-tuned query-local baseline should be close, a two-level decision being within a pointwise
rule's reach; as depth grows the query-local baselines should degrade markedly and MarSea gently.
\emph{Falsification}: a flat gap across depth withdraws the ranked framing for the weaker
binary-exclusivity claim.

\paragraph{Grids.} E2 sweeps $m\in\{1,2,4,8,16\}$ at fixed $n$ and fixed sequence length. E3
sweeps $n$ over five levels up to a few hundred candidates at fixed $m$, each point run twice
(real distractors versus inert padding of equal token count) and at a fixed gold position, with
the position-marginal profile reported alongside. Three seeds on all headline configurations;
mean and standard deviation; matched epochs and schedules across methods. Gains within seed
variance are reported as such. Because the fan-out program couples queries, padding tokens
consume budget unless masked out of the program, and we verify invariance of outputs to padding
length as a correctness check.

\paragraph{Baselines, in full.} Six: unmodified attention; $\softmaxop_1$
\citep{miller2023attention}; a learned-marginal transport in the MESH form; \textbf{an
exclusivity head reading $k_j$ alone}, $\tauK(k_j)$ with $s_{\cdot j}$ and $\nu_j$ withheld;
row-$\alpha$-entmax
\citep{peters2019sparse}; and softmax sparsified to MarSea's realized support size. Each isolates
one design decision and is retuned under a matched schedule rather than quoted from a paper.

\textbf{The third and fourth are the two contribution (i) rests on, and the third is not an
ablation we invented.} \citet{zhang2023mesh} learn \emph{both} marginals, each as
$m\cdot\softmaxop(h(\cdot))$ from a network reading one element at a time, and solve an entropic
transport between them at a global regularization, so the output is dense: read in this paper's
orientation it is a learned $c_j$ and a learned row total with no shape on either margin, the
mirror image of Sec.~\ref{sec:method} (Table~\ref{tab:object}).
Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling} pins its column precision at $m_j/|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$
however well the marginals are learned, so it is the decisive test of whether \emph{shape}, not
capacity, is what moves precision. One limitation of that arm is the baseline's, not ours, and we
state it: under a causal mask a two-marginal transport is generically infeasible --- the last key
is visible to one query, whose row must then carry that key's whole marginal --- so Sinkhorn
cannot satisfy both marginals; we end the iteration on the row scaling, which the value mix needs,
and report the column-marginal residual. The fourth withholds the field from $\tauK$ and isolates the
field argument: Cor.~\ref{cor:necessity} says a head that cannot see the column cannot serve two
keys whose recovery intervals are disjoint, and Fig.~\ref{fig:probe}(b) shows training-free that
the statistic such a head is denied, $\nu_j$, is the one that carries $m_j$. \emph{We predict the
MESH-form arm is the strongest of the six on answer score and sits on the comparator's line in
E3, and that the key-alone arm loses on E7's interval-hit rate.} If the key-alone arm matches
MarSea's hit rate the field argument is decoration; if the MESH-form arm matches MarSea on
exact-set accuracy, shape is not what the task needs, and contribution (i)'s task-level claim is
what fails.

The last two answer the objection that
Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling}'s ceiling is an artifact of full support that any sparsifier
clears. \textbf{The answer differs by margin, and an earlier draft got the row wrong.} On the
\emph{column}, neither row-$\alpha$-entmax nor matched-sparsity softmax enforces a column
constraint, so by Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} their support \emph{membership} is uncontrolled even
where its \emph{size} matches MarSea's, and reporting $P_j$ against them compares mechanisms
rather than sparsity levels. On the \emph{row} that argument does not apply: a query-local
sparsifier controls its own row membership completely, and what separates MarSea from it there
is only \emph{which keys Stage~1 lets reach the row}. We therefore measure that directly rather
than assert it (next paragraph).

\paragraph{The single-target and the empty column are row-side tests, and the split is
reported.} With one answer query, every passage key has $m_j\in\{0,1\}$. At $m_j=1$
Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery} has $W_j=0$: for $\tau_j\ge1/\delta_j$ the key's whole quota goes to its
largest-scoring query and the rest of the column is exactly zero, continuously in $\tau_j$
(Sec.~\ref{sec:method-fanout}). At $m_j=0$ no temperature can empty a simplex, so the key reaches
whichever query scores it highest, $P_j=0$ by definition, and the column is a test of nothing;
\textbf{we declare it a row-side test} and exclude $m_j=0$ columns from every column average,
reporting their count. What the row sees is the composition of the two stages, and E8 splits the
distractor keys of each answer row three ways: \emph{excluded by Stage~1} (their largest-scoring
query is not the answer query, so their entry on the answer row is an exact zero before any row
program runs), \emph{rejected by the row} ($\theta_i$ removed them), and \emph{surviving}. The
first share is the Stage-1-attributable part of MarSea's row precision, and it is the quantity
that separates MarSea from row-$\alpha$-entmax and matched-sparsity softmax on the row margin,
since a query-local sparsifier has no Stage~1 to do it; the harness reports the complementary
share --- keys that concentrate \emph{onto} the answer row and are left to $\theta_i$ --- at
$8.5\%$ of keys under random scores (App.~\ref{app:numerics}). The column theory is exercised on
columns with $m_j\ge2$, keys that govern several annotated queries; where those come from is
stated with E7.

\paragraph{Reporting rules.} Fine-tuned and zero-shot numbers are never pooled: every
comparison is against the same decoder under the same schedule, and a zero-shot figure quoted
from another paper is not a control. Nor do we contest multi-stage retrieve-then-read pipelines
with much larger readers --- MarSea is a change to one normalization step, evaluated as a
mechanism ablation, and a pipeline that beats it is not evidence about the mechanism.

\paragraph{Metrics, and why the task criteria are not recall-only.} Evidence selection is
scored as retrieval against the annotated gold set: support precision, support recall and, where
a graded reading is wanted, target mass, each reported separately and never as a single aggregate
(Prop.~\ref{prop:stagecomp}). \textbf{Task performance is reported on each benchmark's own
precision-sensitive criteria, not only its answer score}, because an answer score is blind to
over-selection and a full-support mechanism's recall is $1$ for free. On HotpotQA we report the
official supporting-fact EM and F1 and the \emph{joint} metrics, $P_{\mathrm{joint}} =
P_{\mathrm{ans}}P_{\mathrm{sup}}$ and likewise for recall, with joint EM demanding both exact
\citep{yang2018hotpotqa}; supporting-fact EM is exact set recovery, which is the task-level image
of $P_j=R_j=1$ and the place the precision claim has to cash out. On MuSiQue-Answerable we report support F1
beside answer F1 \citep{trivedi2022musique}; MuSiQue-Full's paired sufficiency scores are a
different instrument --- a binary judgement of whether a context suffices, scored zero on a pair
if either half is called wrongly --- so we report them as an abstention diagnostic and not as a
precision criterion. \textbf{One granularity caveat}: HotpotQA annotates supporting facts at the
\emph{sentence} level while $m_j$ here is a count of \emph{passages}, so the supporting-fact
metrics are read at the granularity the benchmark annotates and the $m$-sweep at the granularity
the theory is about; we say which is which wherever both appear. RULER is
the exception and it is the one that matters most, since E2 runs there: it ships a
\emph{recall-based} score that checks only for the presence of the target values
\citep{hsieh2024ruler}, so over-selection is free. \textbf{We therefore pre-commit E2 to
exact-set accuracy} --- the returned set equals the gold value set, no distractor value admitted
--- reporting RULER's own score alongside it; needle and distractor identity are configuration
parameters of the generator, so the stricter criterion costs nothing and is the one the theory
speaks to. IHEval is scored as task accuracy, and the depth stratification E6 reports is taken on its
multi-turn rule-following task, which is where the number of conflicting components varies;
elsewhere its conflicts are between two levels \citep{zhang2025iheval}.
Efficiency is reported as parameters, FLOPs, peak memory and wall-clock latency \emph{as a
function of the measured relation coverage} rather than as a single figure, since by
Sec.~\ref{sec:method-gate} the cost of materializing a relation's columns is proportional to it.

\paragraph{E7, E8 and E9 in full.} Wherever the gold evidence set is
annotated, $T_j$ is available directly and E7 reports all five quantities of
Def.~\ref{def:fidelity} --- never a single aggregate --- and recall \emph{twice}, before and
after Stage 2. By Prop.~\ref{prop:stagecomp}(i) recall cannot rise through Stage 2, so any
recall deficit between the two readings is Stage 2's; precision is \emph{not} bracketed the same
way, since a row-indexed floor can remove true edges from a column while leaving false ones in
another row, so we report both precision readings and attribute nothing by subtraction. E7 also
measures $(m_j,\delta_j,W_j)$ from logged scores and reports the distribution of the
\emph{relative interval width} $m_j\delta_j/W_j$ --- Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery} makes the interval
non-empty whenever $\delta_j>0$, so width, not feasibility, is what a hard column costs, and it
is width that Assumption~\ref{ass:margin} turns into a measurement. Since \eqref{eq:tauhead}
reads $s_{\cdot j}$, E7 additionally reports the
\emph{interval-hit rate}: the fraction of active columns whose realized $\tau_j$ lies in
$[1/(W_j+m_j\delta_j),1/W_j)$, together with the signed distance to the nearer endpoint when it
does not. \emph{The sharp endpoints matter here operationally}: scored against the sufficient
interval $[1/(m_j\delta_j),1/W_j)$ a head landing in $[1/(W_j+m_j\delta_j),1/(m_j\delta_j))$
would be recorded as a miss while in fact recovering exactly. This is the most direct test of
Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery} available on real data, and it is
only meaningful because the head sees the column the interval is defined by --- a head reading
$k_j$ alone would be scored against a target it cannot observe. \emph{Falsification}: a hit rate
at chance, with the misses unbiased in sign, says the head is not using the column and the
mechanism is running on a temperature that happens to work rather than one that was inferred.
The same quantity is reported for the row against the interval of
Prop.~\ref{prop:graded}(ii). \textbf{E7 also reports the measured precision--recall trace against
the predicted one.} On a column meeting Assumption~\ref{ass:margin},
Thm.~\ref{thm:recovery} with Lem.~\ref{lem:prefix} and Prop.~\ref{prop:graded}(i) fix the whole
curve in advance, so sweeping $\tau_j$ on held-out columns should reproduce
Fig.~\ref{fig:prcurve} column by column: $R_j=1$ with $P_j=m_j/k^\star(\tau_j)$ below the
interval, $P_j=R_j=1$ on it, $P_j=1$ with $R_j=k^\star(\tau_j)/m_j$ above, and no interior point.
\emph{Be clear about what this does and does not test.} Given the assumption and $\alpha=2$ the
trace is an identity, so a departure indicts the assumption on that column or the
implementation, never the theorem; what it earns is the recall side of the story, which is given
exactly rather than bounded --- $k^\star(\tau_j)/m_j$ on the column, $K/m_j$ under retention
(Prop.~\ref{prop:tournament}(ii)), $k(\theta_i)/|K_i|$ on the row --- and a like-for-like
comparison, since the same sweep run on each baseline shows a full-support mechanism fixed at
$(1,m_j/n_q)$ and a matched-sparsity one moving along no curve the theory predicts, its support
\emph{membership} being uncontrolled by Thm.~\ref{thm:separation}. The genuinely empirical
question remains the interval-hit rate: whether the learned $\tauK$ lands where the column says
it must. E8 logs the relation's coverage on both margins from epoch one --- the fraction of keys with
$\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}\ne\emptyset$, of queries with $\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}\ne\emptyset$, and the
full distributions of $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$ and $|\mathcal{E}_{i\cdot}|$ rather than their
means --- because the degenerate optimum is an empty relation everywhere, which is standard
attention, and the subtler one is a relation of singleton columns, where
Thm.~\ref{thm:separation} says nothing (App.~\ref{app:selective}, cost (a)). It reports
$k^\star$ stratified by $|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$. There is one fan-in trigger: a row enters
\eqref{eq:rowprog} iff its slice of the relation is non-empty, and because the relation is one
object every query a key re-shapes lies in that key's column slice and so has a non-empty row
slice; the row rate is bounded below by the column-induced rate \emph{by construction}, and a
measured violation is an implementation error rather than a finding. It reports the
distribution of $\tau_i\tilde R_i/\bar c_i$ about one, the regime threshold of
Sec.~\ref{sec:method-fanin}, and the correlation between each membership decision and the
realized separation $\delta_j$ of the column it entered, which is how we learn whether the
relation keys on the structure the theory says matters or on something else. Note that $\tauQ$
collapsing to $1$ is \emph{not} the fan-in analogue of an empty relation, since covered rows
still run there and are still capped at one unit; it is the safe default. The failure to watch for is the opposite, $\tau_i$ large enough that rows
are routinely truncated to one or two keys, which would show up as a row-recall deficit in E7. \emph{Falsification}: if $k^\star$ on
covered columns approaches the deployed $K$, the hierarchy is silently truncating and no E7 number
is meaningful until $K$ is raised. \textbf{E9} ablates every design decision in turn --- the relation's parameterization (its rank
$r$, pairwise against key-alone, and which layers carry it), $K$,
block size and emission policy, $\tau_i$ learned against pinned at $1$ (which reduces Stage 2 to
a plain unit cap), $\tau_j$ learned against fixed or uniform, and the inherited capacity of
\eqref{eq:step1} against a uniform one (which is the ablation that puts a predicted budget back
and is reported as such). \textbf{The load-bearing ablation of this group strips the field
argument from each head} --- $\tauK(k_j)$ against $\tauK(k_j,s_{\cdot j},\nu_j)$,
$e_{ij}$ from $u_\phi(k_j)$ alone against the pair, and $\tauK$ with and without $\nu_j$
--- since a mechanism that claims to model a multivariate relation should lose measurably when
its parameters are made blind to the field, and if it does not, the claim is decoration.

\paragraph{E7, the column ground truth.} No benchmark annotates $T_j$, the set of queries that
\emph{should} attend to a key, so E7 derives it by exact string match from annotation the
benchmarks do ship, on both instruments alike, and it uses two kinds of column because the
backbone routes them through different heads. The \emph{coreference column}: a key is the first
token of the \emph{first mention} of a bridge string, and $T_j$ is the first token of every
\emph{later mention} of the same string. On RULER's multi-value task the $m$ needles share one
key phrase, so its first mention has $m+1$ later mentions (the other $m-1$ needles, the
question, and the answer prefix) and $m_j=m+1$ moves with the sweep; on MuSiQue the bridge
strings are the decomposition's intermediate answers, giving a leaf passage $m_j=2$ and a
bridging one $3$--$5$, and because we assemble the prompt the gold passages are placed in hop
order so that every later mention is visible to the earlier one. Reversing that order changes
$m_j$ deterministically, which is a free check that the annotation measures what we say. The
\emph{value column}: a key is a needle's value span and $T_j$ is the answer positions that emit
that value, so $m_j$ is the number of emitting tokens, typically one; this is the column the
answer rows are scored on, and it is the $m_j\le1$ regime of Sec.~\ref{sec:method-fanout}. The
two are not merged into one target set: S0 found that answer positions attend to values and not
to the key phrase's first mention, so a merged $T_j$ would score the coreference column against
a target the backbone never routes to it. Two things are kept separate from both. First, a
target the relation excluded is a \emph{relation recall} miss, logged on its own and never
charged to $\tau_j$; the interval is computed on $T_j\cap\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}$ against the
relation's complement, and a column whose complement is empty has $\delta_j=+\infty$ and a lower
endpoint of $0$. Second, the construction is ground truth about the \emph{task} and a hypothesis
about \emph{routing}, so S0 first checks on the unmodified decoder, sweeping every head, that
some head sends the later mentions to the earlier one --- the mass those queries put on the key
span exceeds half the row's maximum over candidate keys, the sink excluded, on at least $80\%$
of examples --- and a construction that fails the check is reported as not measurable on this
backbone rather than used; the column test runs at the head that routes it, which must be in a
patched layer. Dense arms have no relation, so their column precision is reported on
two domains, the full visible column ($m_j/n$) and MarSea's paired relation
($m_j/|\mathcal{E}_{\cdot j}|$, the identity of Prop.~\ref{prop:fullsupportceiling}); the second
is the headline.

\paragraph{The coverage residual.} Where $T_j$ is annotated, E7 also logs the signed residual
$n_q-\sum_j m_j$: positive when queries go ungoverned, negative when they are claimed by more
than one key. It is a dataset statistic rather than a property of any head, and it is reported
because Cor.~\ref{cor:joint}'s consistency hypothesis is exactly the case where it is zero.

\paragraph{Why these three datasets and not others.} Benchmarks in which one correct answer is
chosen among distractors with no coupling between targets exercise only
Prop.~\ref{prop:scope}(i), the half we concede, and a gain there is evidence for a better scoring
function rather than for Thm.~\ref{thm:separation}; we therefore do not use multiple-choice
reasoning suites as evidence. RULER, MuSiQue and IHEval were selected because each supplies
something the others cannot: a controlled $m$ at constant difficulty, a real task with annotated
gold evidence, and a ranked order over conflicting sources respectively. In real multi-hop data
$m$ is not controlled --- MuSiQue stratifies it by hop count, varying gold-paragraph count and
reasoning depth together, so a gap growing in $m$ there is confounded with ``MarSea helps more on
harder questions'' \citep{min2019compositional}. Neither instrument is reported alone, synthetic
recall being an unreliable predictor of downstream performance \citep{yen2025helmet}.

\end{document}
